WEBVTT

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:50.790
The American experience is made possible by the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation to enhance public understanding of the role of technology.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:57.090
The Foundation also seeks to portray the lives of the men and women engaged in scientific and technological pursuit.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:07.590
At Liberty Mutual Insurance, we do everything we can to help prevent accidents and make America a safer place.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.370
Liberty Mutual is proud to support American experience.

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:20.000
At the Scots Company, we help make gardens more beautiful, lawns greener, trees taller.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:23.920
If there's a better business to be in, please let us know.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.970
American experience is also made possible by the corporate...

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.800
for Public Broadcasting, and contributions to your PBS station from viewers like you.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:36.660
Thank you.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:43.420
Funding for this program was made possible by a grant from the National Endowment for

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:48.210
the Humanities, the Ford Foundation, and by the following.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.500
People were neutral about Eleanor Roosevelt.

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:06.790
People were absolutely drawn to Eleanor Roosevelt.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:12.050
Her presence was felt the minute she came into the room.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:13.510
She sparkled.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:17.350
She had an upcountry aristocratic attitude

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:18.950
that turned a lot of people off.

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:20.910
She had buck teeth.

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:23.890
Her voice kind of quavered

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:26.750
so that it was easy to imitate and to mock.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.380
For more than 30 years, Eleanor Roosevelt was the most powerful woman in America.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:43.520
Niece of one president and wife of another, Eleanor was shaped and driven by politics.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:52.790
One of the things people don't understand about Elle is because she seems so ladylike,

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.030
and she has that aristocratic voice and that manner.

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:59.090
She was tough as nails.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.520
In fact, she was one of the best politicians of the 20th century.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:09.040
We must have equal citizenship for anybody in our country.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:12.400
She was a voice for those who often had none,

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:16.520
but her idealism cost those closest to her dearly.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:18.970
She was a loving person.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:24.600
She may not have been loving enough for her husband, and that was a tragedy.

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:28.560
She was tender to her friends.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:29.980
She may not...

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.830
have been tender enough to her sons and her daughter there's a tremendous amount

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:44.840
of conflict and hurt in her life and a great sense of loss and struggle she was

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:51.010
happiest in the public arena she was least happy in her intimate private life

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:57.920
few people knew the real nature of her marriage to Franklin Roosevelt or of

00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:59.960
the deep friendships she shared with others

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.000
players.

00:04:02.396 --> 00:04:04.616
Determined to live life on her own terms.

00:04:05.084 --> 00:04:08.044
Eleanor Roosevelt traveled far from her beginnings.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.540
to become the most admired.

00:04:10.716 --> 00:04:13.516
and the most controversial woman in America.

00:04:37.836 --> 00:04:42.116
All her life, Eleanor Roosevelt remembered an afternoon from her childhood.

00:04:47.692 --> 00:04:49.132
She was waiting for her father.

00:04:49.932 --> 00:04:52.172
the person she loved most in the world.

00:05:00.252 --> 00:05:01.172
When he arrived,

00:05:01.564 --> 00:05:03.724
She rushed into the warmth of his arms.

00:05:06.076 --> 00:05:09.036
Eleanor delighted in her father's laughter and tenderness.

00:05:10.044 --> 00:05:12.204
and his stories of exotic travel.

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:14.860
of hunting in India.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:17.260
of the beauty of the Taj Mahal.

00:05:19.676 --> 00:05:20.736
One day he promised,

00:05:21.084 --> 00:05:22.144
He would take her there.

00:05:22.812 --> 00:05:24.152
and they would see it together.

00:05:26.908 --> 00:05:28.508
Her father never kept his promise.

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:33.380
But Eleanor treasured the memory of it for the rest of her life.

00:05:44.108 --> 00:05:46.588
Roosevelt was born on October 11th,

00:05:46.668 --> 00:05:50.868
1884, into one of the oldest and wealthiest families in New York.

00:05:53.292 --> 00:05:55.292
She was a sensitive, timid child.

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:59.820
And from her earliest years, Eleanor knew she was a disappointment to her mother.

00:06:01.052 --> 00:06:02.552
Anna Hall Roosevelt.

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:04.620
And I would look it.

00:06:04.828 --> 00:06:06.828
Eleanor sort of coolly.

00:06:07.132 --> 00:06:09.292
and worry that she would never be a beauty.

00:06:10.268 --> 00:06:11.928
because she looked so homely.

00:06:13.148 --> 00:06:15.628
and she would even discuss it in front of

00:06:15.836 --> 00:06:17.836
Eleanor with her friends.

00:06:17.916 --> 00:06:19.076
I don't remember that.

00:06:19.452 --> 00:06:22.452
that she had the feeling from the very beginning, I'm ugly.

00:06:25.884 --> 00:06:26.184
Beautiful.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:29.260
was important in the world in which Eleanor was raised.

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:37.220
In New York high society, girls were brought up to find husbands, have a family, and preside over a household.

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:47.280
Their chief asset was their looks.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.260
If they were beautiful, their lives would be made for them.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:59.880
Anna couldn't imagine having a child that wasn't as vivacious and beautiful as her.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:07.200
She really couldn't understand this shy, awkward little person

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:10.670
whom she called Granny to her face.

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:14.760
Eleanor desperately tried to please her mother, and she did.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:21.700
find one way to do it, which was that her mother was subject to migraines, and Eleanor would come

00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:29.810
and sit and rub her brow for hours, and learn from that that the way to be loved was to be used.

00:07:31.404 --> 00:07:34.344
And I think that was a lesson that stayed with her all her life.

00:07:35.564 --> 00:07:36.244
To Eleanor.

00:07:36.684 --> 00:07:39.664
it seemed that Anna was happier with her two younger brothers.

00:07:40.428 --> 00:07:41.508
Elliot, Jr.,

00:07:41.644 --> 00:07:42.604
and Gracie Hall.

00:07:43.596 --> 00:07:45.596
You

00:07:47.532 --> 00:07:51.332
Everything was different with her father, Elliot, who doted on his daughter.

00:07:54.156 --> 00:07:55.516
charming and popular,

00:07:55.724 --> 00:07:58.724
Eliot was the younger brother of Theodore Roosevelt.

00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:03.650
Like many men of his class, he had no real profession.

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:09.020
He dabbled in real estate, played tennis, and rode to hounds.

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:14.130
Her father made her feel special.

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:18.560
He called her his little Nell, and he made her feel loved.

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:23.020
He didn't make her feel unattractive or shy.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:26.330
She felt very secure in his presence.

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:29.980
But Elliot was...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:40.200
rarely at home. He was an alcoholic, irresponsible, often erratic. He would disappear on drunken

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:48.700
binges for days at a time. Anna tried to shelter Eleanor from his wild behavior, but by August

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:57.400
1892, the family was breaking apart. The Roosevelts feared that Elliot was squandering his inheritance

00:08:57.400 --> 00:08:59.370
and ruining their reputation.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:04.040
They had him confined to a mental institution.

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:10.920
Just four months later, when Eleanor was barely eight years old,

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:14.780
her mother died, suddenly, of diphtheria.

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:21.560
Eleanor felt strangely unmoved.

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:25.230
One fact wiped out everything else, she later wrote.

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:29.060
My father was back, and I would see him soon.

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:42.840
Elliot reappeared briefly and swept Eleanor up in his arms, told her again how wonderful she was

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:47.300
and that everything was going to be all right, and they would go off, and they would take care of her

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:54.470
little brothers, and they would have a family. And so that meant the world to her, and that really

00:09:54.470 --> 00:09:58.060
gave her the hook on which she could hang her life.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.680
considered unfit to care for his children.

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:11.350
The brothers were sent to live with Anna's mother.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:18.280
Grandmother Hall was a widow in her early 50s.

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:23.390
She lived in New York City and spent summers at Tivoli

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:24.860
in upstate New York.

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:27.240
Her grandmother was very religious

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.920
and took her responsibility towards the children.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:38.120
very very seriously but she was stern she was rigid her grandmother spent most of her time in

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:44.860
her room there were these two drunken and and really dangerous uncles one of whom used to

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:51.060
shoot at the neighbors and even at the children with a shotgun from the upstairs window grandmother

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:58.990
hall really imagines that she can raise eleanor and her two brothers differently

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.440
if she is very strict and everything is regimented.

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:06.790
But despite the order and the discipline,

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:08.650
her grandmother did love her

00:11:08.650 --> 00:11:12.550
and gave her a sense of family love.

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:23.050
Eleanor fought the discipline in small ways.

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:27.690
She put hot water into her icy wash basin,

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:29.920
stole candies from the kitchen.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.380
and read in secret on Sundays.

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:38.230
She lived for her father's rare visits.

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:44.900
Sometimes he would promise to come, but not appear.

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:50.760
One day Elliot took her out and stopped at his club.

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:55.710
Saying he would not be long, he left her outside waiting,

00:11:59.160 --> 00:11:59.980
and Eleanor waited.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.140
and waited for hours on the steps.

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:06.910
Finally, the doorman said,

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:10.230
Young lady, who are you waiting for?

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:15.230
And she said, I'm waiting for my father, Mr. Roosevelt.

00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:19.130
And he raised his eyebrows probably,

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:24.880
and he said, Well, young lady,

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:29.960
I think we'd best get you a taxi and send you home because...

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.400
your father left quite a while ago.

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:35.080
What he didn't say is that Elliot Roosevelt

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:38.300
had been put in a taxi dead drunk sometime before.

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:41.870
She wrote to her father frequently,

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:46.540
and she just wanted desperately to go and live with him

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:49.080
and told him in her letters,

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:52.100
please, could she come and take care of him

00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:53.740
and keep house for him?

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:55.040
She was 9 years old.

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:59.780
Eleanor spent much of the summer of 1894

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:13.510
at her grandmother's home in Tivoli, August 13, 1894. Darling little now, what must you think of

00:13:13.510 --> 00:13:20.300
your father who has not written for so long? I have, after all, been very busy, quite ill,

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:27.900
at intervals not able to move from my bed for days. How is your pony, and the dogs at Tivoli too,

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:34.600
tender affection, ever devotedly, your father.

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:40.330
Just hours after writing this, Elliot died.

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:48.240
When she was told of his death,

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:50.690
Eleanor's only words were,

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:53.620
I did want to see father once more.

00:14:08.190 --> 00:14:12.490
After her father's death, Eleanor found comfort in romantic novels

00:14:12.490 --> 00:14:16.090
and in a dream world where her father was still alive.

00:14:16.090 --> 00:14:20.710
As she wandered the woods around Tivoli,

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:24.500
she invented stories of a life together they never had,

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:28.350
where he was the hero and she the heroine.

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:35.010
The memory of her father's love and of its loss

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:38.050
would haunt Eleanor for the rest of her life.

00:14:45.830 --> 00:14:48.910
In 1899, Eleanor turned 15.

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:52.700
Things really do get worse at Tivoli.

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:56.090
Her uncles are more and more out of control.

00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:59.730
At some point, locks appear on her door.

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:05.640
presumably to keep her Uncle Valli, who was an out-of-control alcoholic, out of her room.

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:11.020
Her grandmother thought it would be dangerous to have her in a house she couldn't control,

00:15:11.900 --> 00:15:15.280
where her own grown children were acting so bizarrely.

00:15:16.170 --> 00:15:18.780
She thought it was best to get Eleanor out of there.

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:29.800
Grandmother Hall decided to send Eleanor to England, to a boarding school just outside of London.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:40.690
called Allenswood. Allenswood was run by a charismatic French woman in her late 60s,

00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:48.850
Marie Souvestre. Souvestre was fiercely committed to social and political justice.

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:56.300
Under her influence, girls received a progressive education and were taught to be independent

00:15:56.300 --> 00:15:59.010
and politically aware. Suddenly,

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:03.080
The important things were not the social things.

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:10.690
She was with people who valued her friendship, her loyalty, her intellect.

00:16:11.070 --> 00:16:15.110
Everybody liked her because there was not a mean streak about her.

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:19.200
She was loyal. She always did what she said she would do.

00:16:20.090 --> 00:16:23.220
For the first time in her life, Eleanor belonged.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:38.830
came to her for comfort when they were homesick or needed advice. On weekends, they bought bunches

00:16:38.830 --> 00:16:45.940
of flowers for girls they had crushes on, and Eleanor's room was filled with posies and gifts.

00:17:03.550 --> 00:17:08.440
Most important of all, she became the favorite of Mademoiselle Souveste.

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:13.800
Mademoiselle Souveste saw immediately that here was a very special child, young woman,

00:17:14.320 --> 00:17:18.320
that she was in some ways mature beyond her years,

00:17:18.580 --> 00:17:21.760
but wasn't very knowledgeable about a great many things,

00:17:21.820 --> 00:17:23.740
but there was this thirst for learning.

00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:29.390
My grandmother was absolutely taken by Mademoiselle Souveste because...

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:38.400
She saw this elegant, brilliant woman who was interested in her and what she had to say.

00:17:38.860 --> 00:17:43.100
And she blossomed at Allenswood.

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:48.380
She became the beginnings of the woman that she would become later in life.

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:57.850
During school vacations, Sylvester took Eleanor traveling on the continent.

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:10.200
In Paris, she helped order her first fashionable clothes.

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:20.760
Sylvester encouraged her to visit museums by herself

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:23.920
and to adjust their itinerary to suit their whims.

00:18:32.590 --> 00:18:36.990
Never again, Eleanor wrote, would I be the rigid little person I had been.

00:18:52.150 --> 00:18:57.220
In 1902, after three years at Allenswood, Eleanor returned to New York.

00:18:59.300 --> 00:18:59.940
Theodore Roosevelt...

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.500
was now the President of the United States.

00:19:03.490 --> 00:19:04.770
Boisterous and energetic,

00:19:05.350 --> 00:19:08.230
Uncle Ted always called Eleanor his favorite niece.

00:19:10.210 --> 00:19:13.150
She recalled her childhood visits with him as terrifying.

00:19:14.310 --> 00:19:17.410
He was horrified that I didn't know how to swim, she said.

00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:20.890
So he thought he'd teach me as he taught all his own children,

00:19:20.890 --> 00:19:24.440
and he threw me in, and I sank rapidly to the bottom.

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:28.160
Then he fished me out and lectured me on being frightened.

00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:34.270
Uncle Ted drove home the Roosevelt rule, never show fear.

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:36.740
And like all Roosevelt children,

00:19:37.430 --> 00:19:40.550
Eleanor was taught a strong sense of social responsibility.

00:19:41.210 --> 00:19:43.990
They accepted the servants in the big house

00:19:43.990 --> 00:19:46.110
and their position in society,

00:19:46.110 --> 00:19:50.530
but part of that also was that you owed something back

00:19:50.530 --> 00:19:53.990
to people less fortunate than yourself.

00:19:56.290 --> 00:19:58.650
Eleanor took this sense of duty seriously.

00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:07.700
Twice a week, she rode the public trolley downtown to the grimy, teeming slums of the Lower East Side.

00:20:10.390 --> 00:20:17.720
There, at the university settlement house, she did volunteer work with young immigrants, helping them adapt to life in America.

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:23.660
She taught dance and calisthenics.

00:20:32.810 --> 00:20:35.490
she thought of her work as the highlight of her week.

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:39.980
It was not unusual that society people came from time to time

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:42.480
and looked at what was going on.

00:20:42.830 --> 00:20:46.620
It was unusual that somebody came on a regular basis

00:20:46.620 --> 00:20:48.120
and really worked there

00:20:48.120 --> 00:20:52.680
and considered this a job for which she had taken responsibility.

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:54.500
That was unusual.

00:20:55.880 --> 00:20:58.040
She joined the New York Consumers League,

00:20:58.530 --> 00:20:59.970
an organization which exposed...

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:07.860
harsh working conditions for women and children. She saw things she would never forget. Sweatshops

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:14.080
where women labored long hours for subsistence wages. Tenement homes where children made

00:21:14.080 --> 00:21:29.900
artificial flowers for hours on end until they dropped with exhaustion. Now 18, Ellen

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:39.500
was expected to make her formal debut into society and find a husband. Because she was

00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:44.210
the president's niece, her coming out in 1902 was closely watched.

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:52.230
Hated coming out. She never knew how to do gossip. She just didn't know how to do that.

00:21:53.110 --> 00:21:56.630
She liked to talk about things. She liked to talk about people. She liked to talk about

00:21:56.630 --> 00:21:59.970
what she had read, but just small talk didn't interest.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:00.840
at all, ever.

00:22:04.060 --> 00:22:06.380
Eleanor remembered how awkward she felt.

00:22:06.540 --> 00:22:11.560
The thing you did was so that you would grace society.

00:22:12.350 --> 00:22:14.810
If you were ugly, you tried to make up for it

00:22:14.810 --> 00:22:18.640
by being well-educated and having very good manners.

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:22.850
My aunt didn't think much of herself,

00:22:23.810 --> 00:22:26.050
but she was slim and she was tall,

00:22:26.130 --> 00:22:29.670
and she had a lot of blonde hair.

00:22:30.090 --> 00:22:37.920
done very nicely, and she loved to dance. She danced very well, and she probably cut really

00:22:37.920 --> 00:22:48.650
quite a stunning figure on the ballroom. Several young men soon started to court her. One was her

00:22:48.650 --> 00:22:56.970
distant cousin, Franklin Delano Roosevelt, a 20-year-old student at Harvard. They had known

00:22:56.970 --> 00:22:59.970
each other as small children and had met again.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:01.480
at a party when she was 14.

00:23:03.290 --> 00:23:06.090
He was young and gay and good-looking, she recalled.

00:23:08.080 --> 00:23:09.680
And I was shy and awkward

00:23:09.680 --> 00:23:13.210
and thrilled when he asked me to dance.

00:23:19.670 --> 00:23:23.450
Unlike Eleanor, Franklin had a stable, loving childhood,

00:23:24.050 --> 00:23:26.930
growing up on his parents' estate in Hyde Park, New York.

00:23:28.570 --> 00:23:29.970
He had little contact with him.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:36.250
with other children until he was 14 when he was sent to boarding school. There and at Harvard,

00:23:36.330 --> 00:23:42.060
he had difficulty fitting in, but he learned to hide his feelings behind a charming exterior.

00:23:43.090 --> 00:23:50.430
He was very attractive, very outgoing, a dashing personality, someone who laughed and who was easy

00:23:50.430 --> 00:23:59.010
with people. And she was flattered by the attention, and she fell in love with him. It wasn't hard to do.

00:24:06.200 --> 00:24:10.300
Several times, Franklin met Eleanor after her classes at the settlement house,

00:24:10.300 --> 00:24:14.330
and she introduced him to a world he had never seen.

00:24:18.280 --> 00:24:22.420
Once they helped a girl who was sick back to her dark, crowded tenement home.

00:24:26.850 --> 00:24:28.710
Franklin was shocked by what he saw.

00:24:29.410 --> 00:24:29.970
And afterwards...

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:37.060
kept repeating that he could not believe human beings lived that way. He was fascinated that

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:49.220
a young woman of his social class was the one who was showing him things that moved him. She wasn't

00:24:49.220 --> 00:24:59.970
the light, funny, socialite that people expected he would be interested in. But I think there was

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:09.840
a piece of FDR, a very large piece, that was far more interested in the realities of life.

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:18.030
Franklin proposed in November 1903, and Eleanor immediately accepted.

00:25:21.290 --> 00:25:24.340
He declared himself the happiest man on earth.

00:25:26.800 --> 00:25:29.970
To Franklin, oh darling, I miss you.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:37.660
you so. I long for the happy hours which we have together. I am so happy, so very happy in your love,

00:25:37.820 --> 00:25:45.120
dearest, and all the world has changed for me. If only I can bring to you all that you have brought

00:25:45.120 --> 00:25:54.960
to me, all my dearest wishes will be fulfilled. Goodbye, dearest boy, your devoted little Nell.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:07.440
1905, Eleanor and Franklin were married. President Theodore Roosevelt gave the bride away.

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:15.900
The wedding was over, Eleanor recalled. We suddenly discovered that the minute Uncle Ted left us,

00:26:16.600 --> 00:26:21.870
everybody else left us too. It was really much more important that Uncle Ted was there

00:26:21.870 --> 00:26:29.960
than that we were being married. Eleanor and Franklin's early marriage

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:37.480
life was dominated by another powerful Roosevelt, Franklin's mother, Sarah. Franklin was Sarah's

00:26:37.480 --> 00:26:47.000
only child, and she was extremely possessive and did not want him to go off and marry somebody else

00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:54.130
and have some other life. Eleanor did everything she could to win Sarah's affection. On their

00:26:54.130 --> 00:26:59.820
honeymoon, she wrote her mother-in-law almost daily. Thank you so much.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:01.760
for everything you did for us.

00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:06.560
You are always just the sweetest, dearest mama to your children,

00:27:06.560 --> 00:27:10.310
and I shall look forward to our next long evening together

00:27:10.340 --> 00:27:13.220
when I shall want to be kissed all the time.

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:18.480
She hoped that her mother-in-law would replace her mother.

00:27:18.640 --> 00:27:21.420
She hoped her mother-in-law would love her

00:27:21.420 --> 00:27:26.220
with the unconditional love that she had wanted from her own mother

00:27:26.220 --> 00:27:27.460
but never received.

00:27:28.780 --> 00:27:29.980
But their relationship...

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:31.420
was often strained.

00:27:32.480 --> 00:27:34.680
Sarah tried to run the young couple's life

00:27:34.680 --> 00:27:36.260
as she had Franklin's,

00:27:36.520 --> 00:27:38.220
and she had the power to do it.

00:27:38.800 --> 00:27:40.760
She controlled her son's finances.

00:27:42.140 --> 00:27:44.400
She bought them a townhouse

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:49.100
and also bought the adjoining townhouse for herself

00:27:49.100 --> 00:27:52.530
and had doors built on each floor

00:27:52.530 --> 00:27:54.210
connecting the two houses.

00:27:54.880 --> 00:27:57.880
So there was no privacy from the mother-in-law,

00:27:58.570 --> 00:27:59.890
and the mother-in-law hired this.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:07.950
servants and furnished the house. She was a doer. She wanted Franklin to be part of her world and for

00:28:07.950 --> 00:28:15.970
the most part she got her way. Sarah insisted Eleanor give up her social work and Eleanor

00:28:15.970 --> 00:28:29.730
reluctantly acquiesced. In 1906, the Roosevelt's first child, Anna, was born.

00:28:30.920 --> 00:28:32.440
James was born the next year.

00:28:39.390 --> 00:28:41.250
Eleanor was uneasy in her new role.

00:28:42.580 --> 00:28:45.790
She loved her children, and she wanted to do well,

00:28:46.340 --> 00:28:47.920
but she was not knowledgeable,

00:28:48.660 --> 00:28:51.160
and so she imposed rules on them,

00:28:51.400 --> 00:28:54.660
which even then were not very usual.

00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:57.520
For instance, that she tied the thumbs down

00:28:57.520 --> 00:28:59.300
so that they couldn't suck their thumbs.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:04.710
or she put a cradle out in front of a window, like a window box,

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:09.680
so that the child would get air, which is a scary idea.

00:29:13.420 --> 00:29:18.260
Feelings of insecurity about motherhood only grew with the birth in 1909

00:29:18.260 --> 00:29:20.560
of their third child, Franklin Jr.

00:29:23.680 --> 00:29:28.560
From the beginning, he seemed delicate to have one illness after another.

00:29:30.440 --> 00:29:33.760
When he was just seven months old, he died.

00:29:35.310 --> 00:29:36.790
It was a devastating thing.

00:29:36.890 --> 00:29:39.710
I mean, because she tended to blame herself

00:29:39.710 --> 00:29:42.410
for everything that went wrong in the household.

00:29:43.200 --> 00:29:46.000
So she thought there must have been something she could have done.

00:29:46.140 --> 00:29:49.590
She could have contacted this doctor or that doctor.

00:29:49.930 --> 00:29:52.640
She was really very depressed.

00:29:58.530 --> 00:29:59.970
Elliot was born a year...

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:10.380
later. Sarah hired and fired the nannies. She spoiled the children with treats and comforted

00:30:10.380 --> 00:30:15.680
them when they were hurt. She even told them to think of her as their real mother.

00:30:16.380 --> 00:30:22.710
Your mother only bore you, she said. They learned early that if their mother wouldn't give them

00:30:22.710 --> 00:30:29.880
something, all they had to do was to go see Granny. And they could charm Granny out of anything they want.

00:30:30.250 --> 00:30:34.940
Granny wanted the children to love her as though they were her children.

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:40.080
And my aunt wanted them to grow into good people

00:30:40.080 --> 00:30:45.830
and felt she had to be the disciplinarian and that it wasn't quite fair.

00:30:47.020 --> 00:30:50.300
That must have been very frustrating for a young married woman

00:30:50.300 --> 00:30:55.590
to express her difficulties with the mother-in-law to a man

00:30:55.590 --> 00:30:58.370
who would not confront his mother.

00:30:59.200 --> 00:30:59.980
If something...

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:08.280
was unpleasant, and he didn't want to know about it. He just ignored it. He always thought that

00:31:08.280 --> 00:31:14.920
if you ignored a thing long enough, it could settle itself. Ella Roosevelt is often blamed for being a

00:31:14.920 --> 00:31:23.750
bad mother. Her husband was not a very good father, and expected her and his mother to do

00:31:23.750 --> 00:31:27.950
all the parenting, and he was supposed to come home and have fun with the kids, and he did. They

00:31:27.950 --> 00:31:29.980
adored him, but

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:33.800
When that was over, he wasn't very interested in helping them much.

00:31:34.220 --> 00:31:39.260
And I think the children suffered from the problems both their parents had.

00:31:40.890 --> 00:31:45.350
FDR had trained as a lawyer, but his ambition was to be president.

00:31:46.660 --> 00:31:50.540
He began his political career serving a term in the New York State Senate.

00:31:51.830 --> 00:31:56.730
Then, in 1913, he was appointed Assistant Secretary of the Navy.

00:32:02.130 --> 00:32:04.890
Eleanor moved the family to Washington, D.C.

00:32:07.830 --> 00:32:12.860
She would have two more children, a second Franklin Jr. in 1914

00:32:12.860 --> 00:32:16.080
and John two years later.

00:32:19.970 --> 00:32:22.790
This was a young couple on the move.

00:32:24.590 --> 00:32:27.090
FDR's career was taking off.

00:32:28.480 --> 00:32:29.980
There were a lot of things expected.

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:34.030
of the wife of such an up-and-coming government official

00:32:34.030 --> 00:32:38.360
that she had to do, just out of duty.

00:32:39.400 --> 00:32:43.520
She hired a social secretary, Lucy Mercer, to help her.

00:32:44.440 --> 00:32:47.380
Charming, intelligent, warm, and reliable,

00:32:47.680 --> 00:32:50.820
Lucy fit easily into the bustling Roosevelt household.

00:32:52.430 --> 00:32:54.330
Washington Etiquette required Eleanor

00:32:54.330 --> 00:32:56.230
to make dozens of social visits,

00:32:56.500 --> 00:32:59.900
leaving calling cards at the homes of other officials' wives.

00:33:00.060 --> 00:33:02.080
That was the proper thing to do.

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:05.060
If they had dropped them at your house, you had to return the compliment.

00:33:05.300 --> 00:33:10.520
You had to have an open house day when people came dropped in for tea and that sort of thing.

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:14.840
She really didn't appreciate that. She didn't like it.

00:33:15.160 --> 00:33:19.740
She knew she had to do it, and she did it probably with a rather stiff grace.

00:33:21.450 --> 00:33:24.350
In the evenings, there were cocktails, dinners, and dances.

00:33:25.580 --> 00:33:29.920
Franklin always enjoyed himself. Eleanor often did not.

00:33:31.530 --> 00:33:36.750
My Aunt Eleanor had a distinct aversion to alcohol

00:33:36.750 --> 00:33:40.590
because it affected so many of her family.

00:33:41.310 --> 00:33:44.250
Her father had died of alcoholism.

00:33:44.780 --> 00:33:48.680
Her brother Hall, my father, would die of alcoholism.

00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:52.490
The whole idea of people being out of control terrified her

00:33:52.800 --> 00:33:54.380
and made her terribly uneasy.

00:33:54.880 --> 00:33:57.590
And so she found being at parties

00:33:57.590 --> 00:33:59.970
at which her husband and other people got...

00:34:00.030 --> 00:34:09.540
pleasantly tipsy. It wasn't pleasant to her. He loved having fun. He enjoyed his cocktail hour.

00:34:09.720 --> 00:34:18.610
He enjoyed poker games. He loved good gossip. He liked to flirt with women. He was just amusing

00:34:18.610 --> 00:34:27.320
himself and others. And Mrs. Rosewood got jealous. He, after all, was a man who never really confided,

00:34:27.320 --> 00:34:29.960
never confided in his mother with whom he was so close.

00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:38.960
And she craved intimacy, and that's the one thing that her husband couldn't give her.

00:34:46.660 --> 00:34:51.490
In the summer of 1917, Eleanor took the children north to Campobello,

00:34:52.120 --> 00:34:55.120
the Roosevelt's sprawling summer home off the coast of Maine.

00:35:01.150 --> 00:35:03.390
Franklin stayed behind working in Washington.

00:35:04.610 --> 00:35:05.910
So did Lucy Mercer.

00:35:06.780 --> 00:35:08.700
Mrs. Roosevelt went away for the summer.

00:35:09.930 --> 00:35:13.920
There was Lucy in the house, and there was lonely Franklin.

00:35:15.920 --> 00:35:19.420
And I think this developed the way things do develop,

00:35:20.020 --> 00:35:21.420
with nobody planning them.

00:35:21.940 --> 00:35:22.840
They just happen.

00:35:24.020 --> 00:35:27.460
The next summer, Franklin visited American troops in Europe.

00:35:29.020 --> 00:35:29.980
When he got home...

00:35:30.730 --> 00:35:35.170
Eleanor was left with the job of unpacking his suitcase,

00:35:37.080 --> 00:35:40.000
but in the course of putting his clothes away and so on,

00:35:40.180 --> 00:35:43.480
she came across a little packet of letters.

00:35:45.040 --> 00:35:48.600
And without really wanting to be nosy,

00:35:48.700 --> 00:35:51.980
she couldn't help but see that here was a whole bunch of letters

00:35:51.980 --> 00:35:55.000
between Franklin and Lucy,

00:35:55.920 --> 00:35:59.960
which, upon closer examination, revealed that there was...

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:11.910
something seriously going on here. The way that my Aunt Eleanor felt about Franklin was the way

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:23.020
she had felt about her father. It was a fantastic love that she felt would be total. When she

00:36:23.020 --> 00:36:29.760
discovered that Franklin had an affair, she was so stunned.

00:36:31.180 --> 00:36:34.880
and didn't know where to put this hurt.

00:36:35.880 --> 00:36:40.510
I think the greatest hurt was that Franklin had broken his wood.

00:36:40.670 --> 00:36:45.570
It was like her father, who had made promises and not kept them.

00:36:47.720 --> 00:36:51.170
She confronted him and said, you can have a divorce.

00:36:53.000 --> 00:36:59.850
But at the same time, Sarah was informed and she said, no way, we don't do divorce.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:09.130
in this family. And Franklin, you better straighten up and fly right. Franklin realized a divorced man

00:37:09.130 --> 00:37:18.660
could never be elected president. After he promised never to see Lucy again, Eleanor agreed to go on

00:37:18.660 --> 00:37:28.160
with the marriage. But they never lived together as husband and wife again. And never, in all her

00:37:28.160 --> 00:37:29.970
writings, all her memoirs.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:45.050
articles, and interviews did Eleanor ever mention Franklin's betrayal. A year later,

00:37:45.770 --> 00:37:59.280
Grandmother Hall died. Eleanor went to Tivoli for the funeral. Still devastated by Franklin's affair,

00:37:59.890 --> 00:37:59.970
she...

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:09.790
could not eat. She was lonely and exhausted. Out of her grief, she begins to compare her life to

00:38:09.790 --> 00:38:16.680
her grandmother's life. Her grandmother could have been a painter. Her grandmother could have

00:38:16.680 --> 00:38:23.620
done so much more than she did. And it's very clear to her that being a devoted wife

00:38:23.620 --> 00:38:26.760
and a devoted mother is not enough.

00:38:32.650 --> 00:38:37.610
And Eleanor Roosevelt decides she is going to do everything possible with her life.

00:38:37.780 --> 00:38:40.200
She's going to live a full life.

00:38:49.100 --> 00:38:52.240
Eleanor and Franklin moved back to New York in 1920.

00:38:54.850 --> 00:38:58.910
Eleanor, now 36 years old, embarked on a new life.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:01.640
She took a secretarial course.

00:39:01.980 --> 00:39:05.780
She joined the League of Women Voters and the Women's City Club.

00:39:06.500 --> 00:39:09.940
She went into this work doubting that she could be of much help

00:39:10.450 --> 00:39:16.410
and found that she had a quick mind and people began to appreciate her.

00:39:16.880 --> 00:39:20.650
And when that happened, she began to appreciate herself a little

00:39:20.650 --> 00:39:23.170
and it was kind of a snowballing thing.

00:39:23.680 --> 00:39:26.640
She is a terrific fundraiser and organizer

00:39:26.640 --> 00:39:29.880
and very quickly she meets other women in New York.

00:39:30.440 --> 00:39:39.500
They remind her of the circle that she left at Allenswood, independent women, and it's a world that she relishes and enjoys.

00:39:44.080 --> 00:39:48.940
But almost immediately, the Roosevelts were engulfed in another family crisis.

00:39:49.160 --> 00:39:56.870
In the summer of 1921, they vacationed at Campobello.

00:40:03.040 --> 00:40:04.760
was getting near to supper time,

00:40:07.180 --> 00:40:09.490
Franklin started to go upstairs

00:40:09.490 --> 00:40:11.680
and said his back aches

00:40:11.700 --> 00:40:13.080
and he didn't feel very well.

00:40:17.870 --> 00:40:19.450
And by the next morning,

00:40:19.970 --> 00:40:22.170
he could hardly stand.

00:40:23.550 --> 00:40:24.370
By the next day,

00:40:24.550 --> 00:40:25.810
he could not stand at all.

00:40:29.180 --> 00:40:29.960
My grandmother...

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:34.520
She was terrified for him. She wasn't really sure what it was.

00:40:35.370 --> 00:40:39.900
Would he live? Would he die? Would he ever walk again?

00:40:40.480 --> 00:40:45.100
She went into action and she just did everything she could to keep him alive,

00:40:45.100 --> 00:40:51.420
to bring his fever down, somehow try to make him comfortable, to change his bedclothes.

00:40:51.700 --> 00:40:56.130
At the same time, she has five children who are saying,

00:40:56.340 --> 00:40:59.950
what's wrong, what's wrong? They knew something terrible.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:13.020
was happening. Franklin's illness was diagnosed as polio. His legs were left

00:41:13.020 --> 00:41:18.470
withered and useless. He had to be carried off the island to return to New

00:41:18.470 --> 00:41:29.790
York. There was a real battle in the Roosevelt.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:38.310
family over Franklin's future. His mother thought that he should come home to Hyde Park

00:41:38.310 --> 00:41:45.380
and become a country gentleman and be a happy invalid. He didn't want to do that. He loved

00:41:45.380 --> 00:41:52.250
Hyde Park, but he didn't want to be there forever. Eleanor Roosevelt backed him. She felt that if

00:41:52.250 --> 00:41:57.780
he wanted to try to get back into politics, he should be allowed to try. Eleanor didn't want

00:41:57.780 --> 00:41:59.960
to go back to the country with an...

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:04.220
after she had come that far to free herself.

00:42:05.110 --> 00:42:07.010
Bringing him back into functioning

00:42:07.010 --> 00:42:09.680
was part of bringing herself back

00:42:09.680 --> 00:42:11.900
into an independent, functioning person.

00:42:12.940 --> 00:42:15.780
Sarah and Eleanor were each certain they were right

00:42:15.780 --> 00:42:17.980
and acting in Franklin's best interests.

00:42:19.420 --> 00:42:22.920
In her distress, Eleanor became cold and silent.

00:42:24.780 --> 00:42:26.440
In the spring of 1922,

00:42:27.020 --> 00:42:29.980
the atmosphere in the Roosevelt House was filled with tenures.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:51.770
One day as Eleanor was reading to her younger sons, she broke down.

00:42:53.500 --> 00:42:54.540
The family was stunned.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:17.720
fled to a quiet room for the first time in her life she could not stop crying she just breaks

00:43:17.720 --> 00:43:25.880
down it's a warning to her she's really exhausted and she's gonna have to get out there and heal

00:43:26.000 --> 00:43:29.980
herself as well as the

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:36.270
crisis of FDR's illness subsided, Eleanor and Franklin moved more and more in separate worlds.

00:43:37.910 --> 00:43:44.050
He spent most of his time in the South, first in Florida and later in Warm Springs, Georgia,

00:43:44.840 --> 00:43:50.600
trying to regain the use of his legs. He was still determined to run for president one day.

00:43:52.450 --> 00:43:57.800
His secretary, Missy LeHan, stayed with him and was now his closest companion.

00:44:00.220 --> 00:44:01.640
Eleanor remained in New York.

00:44:02.430 --> 00:44:05.750
She did not share Franklin's belief that he would one day walk again,

00:44:05.750 --> 00:44:08.740
but she did not try to make him come home.

00:44:09.570 --> 00:44:13.370
I think they found life apart easier than life together.

00:44:16.630 --> 00:44:19.730
Both of them had causes to which they could devote themselves.

00:44:21.040 --> 00:44:24.640
They would come together periodically and then float apart again.

00:44:26.580 --> 00:44:28.950
The Roosevelt's remained very fond of one another.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:38.960
that's the way that they made their marriage work. Eleanor tried to make up for Franklin's absence.

00:44:40.780 --> 00:44:48.490
She tried to be more open with her children, especially Anna. She even learned to swim and to

00:44:48.490 --> 00:44:58.400
play with her younger boys. And she threw herself into politics. She was helped by FDR's closest

00:44:58.400 --> 00:44:59.960
advisor, Louis Howe.

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:06.520
A former journalist, Howe was a chain smoker whose clothes were always covered in ash.

00:45:07.360 --> 00:45:12.890
He was untidy and disheveled, and he had one of the shrewdest political minds in America.

00:45:13.850 --> 00:45:21.450
After the polio attack, Louis Howe was always convinced that Franklin's political career must be continued.

00:45:22.170 --> 00:45:29.920
And he decided that I should work with the Democratic Party and keep the contacts alive for Franklin.

00:45:30.060 --> 00:45:36.600
In the spring of 1922, she was asked to address a Democratic Party fundraiser.

00:45:37.490 --> 00:45:42.390
She was terrified of speaking in public, but Louie Howe encouraged her to accept.

00:45:43.100 --> 00:45:46.980
Eleanor Roosevelt really does not like it for us to speak in public.

00:45:47.140 --> 00:45:52.630
And she has a very high, uncontrollable voice that goes up and down.

00:45:52.890 --> 00:45:57.030
And then, because she's nervous, she laughs at the wrong times.

00:45:57.230 --> 00:45:59.510
And Louie Howe would sit in the back.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:06.120
and make faces and gestures and get that voice low and get it under control.

00:46:06.550 --> 00:46:10.290
And he watches every word, and they write her speeches together.

00:46:10.610 --> 00:46:19.580
The early 1920s were a contradictory time for American women.

00:46:21.610 --> 00:46:23.410
They had won the battle for suffrage,

00:46:25.460 --> 00:46:29.960
but women who wanted social reforms still had trouble making their demeanor.

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:39.590
man's herd by the men who ran party politics. She got together with some other very capable women

00:46:39.590 --> 00:46:47.960
and they insisted on a role for women in the Democratic Party. She was out there on the front

00:46:47.960 --> 00:46:55.080
lines of politics, helping women organize in labor unions, deal with abuses in the workplace,

00:46:55.510 --> 00:46:59.580
child labor. In 1924, as elections approached,

00:47:00.320 --> 00:47:05.280
Eleanor was determined that she and her colleagues would have more than a token presence at the convention.

00:47:06.420 --> 00:47:09.920
Charles Murphy was the boss of the Democratic Party in New York.

00:47:10.280 --> 00:47:16.520
And he insisted that he be allowed to choose the women delegates to the coming convention.

00:47:17.110 --> 00:47:20.030
And Mrs. Roosevelt insisted that women would choose them.

00:47:20.740 --> 00:47:25.260
And in the most genteel and polite and ladylike way,

00:47:25.260 --> 00:47:29.960
she suggested that if he didn't give in to her, she would have to go to the convention.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:38.970
press. Charles Murphy held his ground and Eleanor carried out her threat. Women must gain the

00:47:38.970 --> 00:47:45.380
respect of men, she declared in a blunt speech. We will be enormously strengthened if we can show

00:47:45.380 --> 00:47:52.430
that we are willing to fight to the very last ditch for what we believe in. The battle made

00:47:52.430 --> 00:47:59.760
front page news. The boss caved. It was really her first taste of political.

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:05.080
but she had beaten a formidable foe right out of the box.

00:48:05.480 --> 00:48:09.140
And she had enormous pleasure in reporting this to Franklin,

00:48:09.260 --> 00:48:10.260
that she'd beaten this man.

00:48:10.980 --> 00:48:16.470
The cover for it, the line, which she herself put forth all the time,

00:48:16.610 --> 00:48:21.450
was that she was only doing it for the sake of Franklin and Franklin's career.

00:48:22.050 --> 00:48:26.280
But in fact, she was enjoying it.

00:48:34.240 --> 00:48:42.020
Through her work, Eleanor made a circle of close friends, politically sophisticated,

00:48:42.400 --> 00:48:45.140
independent women like Nancy Cook.

00:48:48.970 --> 00:48:52.870
Cook was a creative, energetic organizer in the state Democratic Party.

00:48:54.700 --> 00:48:57.820
She lived in Greenwich Village with her partner, Marion Dickerman.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:04.750
Dickerman was a teacher and the first woman to run for the New York State Legislature.

00:49:05.320 --> 00:49:12.190
When Dickerman took over the private Todd Hunter School in New York,

00:49:12.920 --> 00:49:16.460
Eleanor joined her, teaching literature and history three days a week.

00:49:16.920 --> 00:49:18.980
She was a fascinating teacher.

00:49:19.960 --> 00:49:26.210
I was not very good at math, and I was about to take college boards,

00:49:26.350 --> 00:49:29.830
so the school suggested I drop.

00:49:30.220 --> 00:49:34.220
Mrs. Roosevelt's course, English, and take more math.

00:49:34.630 --> 00:49:39.490
I said, no, I do not want to give up Mrs. Roosevelt's class.

00:49:44.810 --> 00:49:48.950
Franklin supported Eleanor's independence and enjoyed her new friends.

00:49:53.820 --> 00:49:59.980
In 1925, he even built them a small stone house called Val Kill near his mother's house.

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:08.930
Hyde Park estate. My Aunt Eleanor had never had a home of her own. She had always longed for one.

00:50:09.130 --> 00:50:14.940
I mean, here was a grown-up woman with five children and a husband, and she never had lived

00:50:14.940 --> 00:50:24.680
in her own house, and this was her dream. It was an unusual arrangement. Sometimes Franklin joined

00:50:24.680 --> 00:50:29.400
them for meals and picnics. When occasion demanded,

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:32.900
Eleanor would entertain with Franklin at Sarah's house nearby.

00:50:33.000 --> 00:50:37.500
But Val Kill was Eleanor's,

00:50:38.320 --> 00:50:41.500
and she would think of it as her real home for the rest of her life.

00:50:44.480 --> 00:50:48.920
Within her private world at Val Kill, with very, very close friends,

00:50:49.360 --> 00:50:51.900
she clearly was having a wonderful time.

00:50:52.600 --> 00:50:56.200
And part of her joy is the fact that she's having a good time.

00:50:57.680 --> 00:50:59.890
She is surprised and astonished.

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:01.820
and delighted to be having a wonderful time.

00:51:04.320 --> 00:51:23.120
By 1928, Eleanor was director of the Bureau of Women's Activities for the Democratic Party

00:51:23.120 --> 00:51:27.950
and one of the most powerful and well-known women in national politics.

00:51:27.990 --> 00:51:29.960
She published...

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:36.680
articles in major magazines on everything from parenting to foreign policy to the changing role

00:51:36.680 --> 00:51:43.160
of women in society. She was asked to endorse products, and her first ads for the Simmons

00:51:43.160 --> 00:51:51.960
Mattress Company appeared in Vogue magazine. That same year, FDR decided to re-enter politics

00:51:51.960 --> 00:51:59.390
and run for governor of New York. To dispel rumors that he was still sick, he ran an energetic campaign

00:52:00.440 --> 00:52:07.970
and he won. For the first time in nearly a decade, Eleanor was a political wife again.

00:52:12.980 --> 00:52:18.480
She moved the family to Albany and divided her time between her duties as the governor's wife

00:52:18.480 --> 00:52:26.480
and her own activities. Franklin and I had a desire to see improvements for people.

00:52:27.220 --> 00:52:29.960
I knew about social conditions perhaps more than...

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:33.300
and he did, but he knew about government

00:52:33.300 --> 00:52:37.020
and how you could use government to improve certain things.

00:52:37.280 --> 00:52:42.770
And I think we began to get an understanding of teamwork.

00:52:46.340 --> 00:52:49.610
FDR could not walk, and it was difficult for him

00:52:49.610 --> 00:52:51.910
to go inside the schools, hospitals,

00:52:51.970 --> 00:52:54.150
and state institutions he wanted to visit.

00:52:55.130 --> 00:52:58.030
Franklin started training me as an observer.

00:52:59.030 --> 00:52:59.970
He sent me in the first...

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:03.760
This time, afterwards, he began to ask me questions.

00:53:04.640 --> 00:53:06.160
What was the food like?

00:53:06.680 --> 00:53:11.130
And I said, oh, I looked at the menus, and they seemed very adequate.

00:53:11.390 --> 00:53:13.950
And he said, I didn't ask you about the menus.

00:53:13.950 --> 00:53:15.970
I asked you what the food was like.

00:53:16.190 --> 00:53:18.730
You should have looked in the pots on the stove.

00:53:19.530 --> 00:53:24.380
And after that, I was very much better as an inspector.

00:53:25.550 --> 00:53:29.920
They were able to forge a partnership, and through that partnership,

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:39.380
they became closer. It took time, but I think they came to the realization that their love

00:53:39.380 --> 00:53:44.480
for each other truly hinged on values that were very deep.

00:53:45.980 --> 00:53:53.670
We older people must not try to make the younger generation do things the way we did them.

00:53:54.170 --> 00:53:59.980
Eleanor was 46 years old. Her poise in front of the cameras was new and striking.

00:54:04.220 --> 00:54:12.890
The change was partly due to the encouragement of Earl Miller, a New York state trooper whom

00:54:12.890 --> 00:54:14.790
Franklin assigned to be her bodyguard.

00:54:15.800 --> 00:54:19.420
Miller saw that she was sometimes still overcome by shyness.

00:54:20.630 --> 00:54:26.070
Smile just for one picture, he would coax her, often clowning behind the camera to make

00:54:26.070 --> 00:54:26.730
her relax.

00:54:29.340 --> 00:54:29.980
Miller was...

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:36.040
amateur boxer and a talented athlete. He encouraged her to take up riding a game.

00:54:45.020 --> 00:54:53.370
He helped her swim better, drive better. He even taught her to shoot. He boosted

00:54:53.370 --> 00:54:59.830
her confidence and made her laugh. People gossiped about Eleanor and Earl.

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:02.620
as they did about FDR and Missy LeHan.

00:55:04.260 --> 00:55:06.140
Miller always dismissed the rumors.

00:55:07.290 --> 00:55:10.570
You don't go to bed with someone you call Mrs. Roosevelt, he said.

00:55:10.670 --> 00:55:18.300
In condition that you'll come back and visit me in January,

00:55:18.770 --> 00:55:20.870
at least I can give you some very wonderful scenery.

00:55:21.090 --> 00:55:22.350
Either Albany or Washington.

00:55:22.960 --> 00:55:26.740
Well, I think for Washington, we might almost toss a coin.

00:55:28.800 --> 00:55:29.980
It was Louis Howe.

00:55:30.250 --> 00:55:35.590
not Franklin, who told Eleanor her husband was going to run for president in 1932.

00:55:37.460 --> 00:55:40.120
She had known for years that this was his ambition,

00:55:40.840 --> 00:55:44.800
but the news threw her into a depression she could barely hide in public.

00:55:46.010 --> 00:55:49.810
The entire Roosevelt family was thrust into the national spotlight.

00:55:50.490 --> 00:55:54.700
My granddaughter, Anna Roosevelt Dow, on her lap.

00:55:55.760 --> 00:55:57.440
What's our campaign slogan, Sissy?

00:56:00.800 --> 00:56:25.660
good that's right Eleanor dreaded the idea of being first lady of a life

00:56:25.660 --> 00:56:27.780
defined by teas and receiving lines

00:56:30.350 --> 00:56:36.350
even as the governor's wife she had still quite an independent life and she was a bit distraught

00:56:36.350 --> 00:56:50.090
about the idea of being totally immersed in this goldfish bowl of the white house the day she

00:56:50.090 --> 00:56:59.080
realized that she was going to be the wife of the president was a traumatic day for her i knew what

00:56:59.080 --> 00:56:59.980
traditionally would like.

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:08.870
before me, Eleanor remembered, and I cannot say I was pleased with the prospect. The turmoil in my

00:57:08.870 --> 00:57:29.960
heart and mind was rather great that night. In 1933, when Franklin Roosevelt

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:34.130
became president, America was paralyzed by the Great Depression.

00:57:36.750 --> 00:57:42.950
Millions had lost their jobs, their homes, and their trust in government.

00:57:48.770 --> 00:57:51.430
With his buoyant smile and boundless confidence,

00:57:52.140 --> 00:57:54.700
Roosevelt made Americans feel he understood them

00:57:54.700 --> 00:57:57.020
and that he would make their lives better.

00:57:58.420 --> 00:57:59.980
He would lead the country out of the country.

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:07.910
crisis with an immediate progressive plan of action. Eleanor was still struggling with her

00:58:07.910 --> 00:58:15.840
new role as First Lady. Desperate for something useful to do, she even offered to help Franklin

00:58:15.840 --> 00:58:24.180
with his mail. He refused, saying it would undermine his secretary, Missy LeHan. Worse,

00:58:24.590 --> 00:58:29.490
he asked her to resign from teaching and from the political activities she loved.

00:58:31.210 --> 00:58:37.790
Mrs. Franklin D. Roosevelt is surprising Washington folks by taking her initial horseback ride as the First Lady of the Land.

00:58:38.550 --> 00:58:43.780
As she searched for a meaningful role, newsreel crews seemed to follow her every move.

00:58:43.920 --> 00:58:49.730
On her favorite mare, Dot, she is going for a canter in the park with Mrs. Henry Morgenthau, Jr. as her companion.

00:58:54.800 --> 00:58:59.740
The press described a White House that was full of energy, teeming with Roosevelt children.

00:59:00.000 --> 00:59:08.430
grandchildren. But to Eleanor, it seemed her life and family were falling apart. Her daughter Anna

00:59:08.430 --> 00:59:14.790
was in the midst of a divorce and moved into the White House with her two children. Third son,

00:59:15.010 --> 00:59:23.380
Elliot, was leaving his wife, Betty. Now, one of Eleanor's Griselda moods, as she called them,

00:59:23.560 --> 00:59:28.890
threatened to overwhelm her. If anyone looks at me, she wrote, I want to weep.

00:59:30.730 --> 00:59:59.900
my mind goes round and round like a squirrel in a cage i want to run and i can't and i despise myself she confided these feelings to the woman who had become her closest friend lorena hickock or hick as eleanor

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:08.720
called her. Like Eleanor, Hick had a traumatic childhood. Her father beat her regularly. After

01:00:08.720 --> 01:00:14.600
her mother died, she ran away from home. She became a top reporter for the Associated Press.

01:00:16.040 --> 01:00:20.060
During the presidential campaign, she was assigned to cover the candidate's wife,

01:00:20.580 --> 01:00:26.620
and she fell passionately in love with Eleanor. Hickok is entirely and totally devoted to her,

01:00:26.760 --> 01:00:29.340
and that Mrs. Roosevelt had never had in her life.

01:00:30.060 --> 01:00:32.900
No one else had been fully devoted to her.

01:00:33.020 --> 01:00:37.460
Not her parents, not her husband, not her children, not her grandmother.

01:00:38.570 --> 01:00:40.420
She had always been on her own.

01:00:41.230 --> 01:00:43.030
Hick was a single woman.

01:00:43.370 --> 01:00:45.820
She didn't have her own attachments.

01:00:46.120 --> 01:00:49.710
She needed Eleanor as much as Eleanor needed her.

01:00:50.230 --> 01:00:56.960
These were two needy people, and they discovered that they could fulfill each other's needs.

01:00:58.340 --> 01:00:59.780
Hick hated to be photographed.

01:01:00.640 --> 01:01:04.240
and she tried to hide from the cameras that followed the first lady everywhere.

01:01:05.840 --> 01:01:10.710
She felt her love for Eleanor compromised her as a journalist, and she gave up her job.

01:01:13.000 --> 01:01:15.120
Eleanor clearly loved Hick in return.

01:01:17.390 --> 01:01:22.410
Hick, darling, all day I've thought of you, and another birthday I will be with you.

01:01:24.060 --> 01:01:26.540
Tonight you sounded so far away and formal.

01:01:27.920 --> 01:01:29.860
Oh, I want to put my arms around you.

01:01:30.220 --> 01:01:32.530
I ache to hold you close.

01:01:33.390 --> 01:01:34.910
Your ring is a great comfort.

01:01:35.780 --> 01:01:39.790
I look at it and think, she does love me, or I wouldn't be wearing it.

01:01:44.310 --> 01:01:52.060
It shows that she was certainly capable of a very intense emotional relationship

01:01:52.060 --> 01:01:57.150
and expressing great love and being there for someone else

01:01:57.150 --> 01:01:59.570
and expecting someone else to be there for her.

01:02:00.700 --> 01:02:07.300
And so, in that way, she developed emotional capacity,

01:02:08.150 --> 01:02:10.550
which had not always been there.

01:02:10.830 --> 01:02:12.030
Hick was a lesbian,

01:02:14.010 --> 01:02:17.640
and Mrs. Roosevelt was very affectionate

01:02:17.640 --> 01:02:19.020
and quite demonstrative,

01:02:19.180 --> 01:02:21.220
not only to Hick, to other women, to men.

01:02:21.800 --> 01:02:23.060
She showed her warmth.

01:02:23.630 --> 01:02:25.470
She definitely was not a lesbian.

01:02:26.740 --> 01:02:29.880
I have no idea whether Marina...

01:02:30.000 --> 01:02:36.460
Hickok had a homosexual relationship with my grandmother or not. And my feeling about that

01:02:36.710 --> 01:02:45.130
is kind of, who cares? They were very good friends. And if they could make each other

01:02:45.410 --> 01:02:50.270
happy in any way, then that's what's important.

01:02:51.790 --> 01:02:59.970
In February 1934, Latour...

01:03:00.000 --> 01:03:03.260
Two women went on a fact-finding trip and holiday to the Caribbean.

01:03:04.620 --> 01:03:08.200
Several women journalists accompanied them and filmed part of the trip.

01:03:10.640 --> 01:03:12.320
Eleanor had never looked happier.

01:03:26.470 --> 01:03:29.360
Hick helped Eleanor define her role as First Lady.

01:03:30.250 --> 01:03:32.190
She taught her how to work with the press.

01:03:36.970 --> 01:03:40.070
Together, they orchestrated one photo opportunity after another.

01:03:48.490 --> 01:03:57.200
In San Juan, Puerto Rico, when they toured the city's worst slums,

01:03:57.820 --> 01:03:59.880
Eleanor told the photographer to take her picture.

01:04:02.040 --> 01:04:05.160
I want the American people to see what it's really like here.

01:04:06.950 --> 01:04:11.900
The trip was a public relations coup for Eleanor, and by extension, for Franklin.

01:04:16.460 --> 01:04:21.560
For two weeks solid, the papers carried stories about her and the president's concern for the region.

01:04:27.380 --> 01:04:29.980
When Eleanor and Hick returned, Franklin met...

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:37.100
him at the station. It was, the press noted, the Roosevelt's 29th wedding anniversary.

01:04:43.750 --> 01:04:50.970
At Hicks' suggestion, Elena held press conferences for women only. She urged her husband to appoint

01:04:50.970 --> 01:04:58.830
women to government positions. She argued that everyone, young people, women, and African-Americans

01:04:58.830 --> 01:04:59.870
should be included.

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:02.700
in FDR's programs putting people back to work.

01:05:05.850 --> 01:05:09.370
She was soon recognized as a new kind of first lady.

01:05:11.860 --> 01:05:14.660
Which we will be encouraging.

01:05:15.310 --> 01:05:19.000
Eleanor Roosevelt had phenomenal, unprecedented energy.

01:05:21.680 --> 01:05:26.410
She was in action, in motion, it seems, 24 hours a day.

01:05:31.120 --> 01:05:34.270
That allowed her to do enormous amounts in her life.

01:05:36.310 --> 01:05:44.840
It was also probably evidence of someone who is terrified to sit still and be alone

01:05:44.840 --> 01:05:47.910
for fear depression will just blanket them.

01:05:49.820 --> 01:05:54.860
She did have depressive bouts, and I think she fended them off wonderfully

01:05:54.860 --> 01:05:58.060
by this ceaseless, ceaseless activity.

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:10.240
In one three-month period, she logged 40,000 miles, giving lectures, visiting schools and factories, opening fairs.

01:06:12.210 --> 01:06:17.690
Six days a week, no matter where she was, she wrote a newspaper column called My Day.

01:06:19.120 --> 01:06:21.180
She talked to people from all walks of life.

01:06:23.920 --> 01:06:29.940
She saw firsthand the new government programs at work and reported back to F.

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:38.920
Every time we had meetings with Mrs. Roosevelt, we'd all sit back and think that we were going to get told how wonderful we were.

01:06:39.290 --> 01:06:42.290
And that was just perfunctory. That's good. That's fine. That's fine.

01:06:42.470 --> 01:06:44.500
But now let's see what else we can do.

01:06:44.880 --> 01:06:47.020
And she did prod us and push us.

01:06:47.590 --> 01:06:49.710
And she made us all reach for the stars.

01:06:50.940 --> 01:06:56.660
And after one of these meetings, we'd all think about how she would go back and say to the president,

01:06:56.860 --> 01:06:59.850
Now, Franklin, these people need more money.

01:07:00.000 --> 01:07:02.400
and you've got to be sure that they're in the budget.

01:07:03.260 --> 01:07:05.960
And we all imagined that she was just giving him fits.

01:07:15.470 --> 01:07:18.280
In her travels, Eleanor saw how the Depression

01:07:18.280 --> 01:07:21.120
had devastated entire regions and industries,

01:07:22.440 --> 01:07:26.020
like Scott's Run, a mining community in West Virginia.

01:07:28.590 --> 01:07:29.970
The miners were...

01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:37.280
half starved for several years living with their children who were half starved and they had

01:07:37.280 --> 01:07:41.700
absolutely nowhere they could go. They had no way to get out of it. For to wash our clothes,

01:07:42.060 --> 01:07:47.310
we caught rain water if it rained. The barrel was outside, that's how you wash dishes, that's how

01:07:47.310 --> 01:07:55.310
you got water, take your bath. For to drink, there was a well up on the hill. It had sulfur water

01:07:55.310 --> 01:07:57.130
and it tasted like rotten eggs.

01:07:58.960 --> 01:07:59.820
It was like...

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:08.040
You existed, yes. We existed, but it wasn't a very pleasant one.

01:08:11.040 --> 01:08:14.600
Eleanor visited Scott's run and was moved by the miners' plight.

01:08:16.500 --> 01:08:21.880
She thought they were perfect candidates for FDR's new Subsistence Homesteads program.

01:08:23.580 --> 01:08:27.980
The program aimed to ease rural poverty by building planned communities

01:08:27.980 --> 01:08:29.960
where people would farm small...

01:08:30.000 --> 01:08:36.700
plots and work in factories nearby. The communities would provide health care and progressive schools.

01:08:45.420 --> 01:08:53.190
In 1933, families from Scott's Run began construction on the program's first community, called Arthurdale.

01:08:55.760 --> 01:08:59.980
With Eleanor as their champion, they built dozens of clean modern houses.

01:09:00.000 --> 01:09:03.520
homes, each with a garden large enough to grow vegetables.

01:09:04.490 --> 01:09:09.930
A year later, the first families moved in.

01:09:13.920 --> 01:09:15.800
That day changed our life completely.

01:09:17.380 --> 01:09:24.620
There was our little white house set against this backdrop of green trees and green grass

01:09:25.760 --> 01:09:29.980
and everything was nice and white and clean.

01:09:30.000 --> 01:09:36.100
and there was a bathroom, and of all things, a bathroom, and it was ours.

01:09:37.660 --> 01:09:43.760
Arthur Dale, Eleanor hoped, would show that people could lift themselves out of poverty if given a chance.

01:09:49.250 --> 01:09:52.310
She came very often, I would say average once a month.

01:09:54.080 --> 01:09:57.040
She knew them immensely well.

01:09:57.790 --> 01:09:59.770
Mrs. Dick Golier would come up to her.

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:07.630
the homesteaders. Hello, Ms. Roosevelt. Oh, hello, Ms. DeGaulier. How are you doing? Your son, he had

01:10:07.630 --> 01:10:15.230
the measles when I was last here. She was on a one-to-one warm basis. She had no side. She was

01:10:15.230 --> 01:10:29.830
what I call old shoe. I tried to use her connections to bring in industry. She even paid teacher

01:10:30.000 --> 01:10:38.520
salaries herself, but the project had not been fully thought through. The homesteaders

01:10:38.520 --> 01:10:45.410
could not grow enough to feed themselves. Business failed to take root, and many residents

01:10:45.410 --> 01:10:55.500
remained on relief. By the early 1940s, Congress lost interest, and federal aid to Arthur Dale

01:10:55.500 --> 01:10:59.960
ended. Eleanor Roosevelt learned that she could not

01:11:00.000 --> 01:11:05.660
Not just because she was nominally in charge, she could not make some things happen.

01:11:05.880 --> 01:11:06.960
And it doesn't work.

01:11:07.600 --> 01:11:13.140
And she really learned about the limits of power and influence from Arthur Dale.

01:11:20.290 --> 01:11:23.870
In 1936, FDR ran for a second term.

01:11:25.470 --> 01:11:29.980
His opponent, Republican candidate Alf Landon, attacked New Deal programs.

01:11:30.660 --> 01:11:35.020
A lot of the money spent in the name of relief has nothing to do with relief.

01:11:35.730 --> 01:11:44.030
And a lot of money has been wasted.

01:11:45.860 --> 01:11:49.340
Eleanor's activist role as First Lady became a campaign issue.

01:11:51.880 --> 01:11:54.600
Newsreels contrasted her to Mrs. Alf Landon.

01:11:58.300 --> 01:11:59.980
Mrs. Landon, Republicans are sh-

01:12:00.000 --> 01:12:13.000
voters was a traditional wife and mother they would refer to a bit of that man in

01:12:13.000 --> 01:12:17.320
the White House was the least unflattering thing they said about it

01:12:17.320 --> 01:12:21.470
In olden days, a glimpse stocking was a little dawn

01:12:21.470 --> 01:12:27.260
of something shocking, but now, God knows, anything goes.

01:12:28.090 --> 01:12:29.970
And she, I think, some...

01:12:30.000 --> 01:12:36.660
people thought she was a busybody and they thought of the roosevelts as more or less traitors to

01:12:36.660 --> 01:12:43.860
their class the world has gone mad today and good's bad today and black's white today and day's nice

01:12:43.860 --> 01:12:51.200
today it has been said that eleanor roosevelt uh viewed the world as one vast slum project

01:12:51.200 --> 01:12:56.170
she was always sitting around here and there coming to some community whose condition she

01:12:56.170 --> 01:12:59.550
didn't like and tutting about it and insisting that something must be done.

01:13:00.000 --> 01:13:05.300
She seemed to have a large political equivalent of the housewife's desire to redecorate.

01:13:06.580 --> 01:13:10.080
Eleanor's visit to a mine was satirized in a famous cartoon.

01:13:12.630 --> 01:13:19.660
It was indicated to me, she responded, that there was certainly something the matter with a woman who wanted to see so much and know so much.

01:13:20.850 --> 01:13:27.020
Later, she added, every woman in public life needs to develop skin as tough as rhinoceros hide.

01:13:28.080 --> 01:13:29.660
So misses all.

01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:33.480
with all her trimmings can broadcast the bed from Stimmons

01:13:33.480 --> 01:13:37.890
cause Franklin knows anything goes.

01:13:41.830 --> 01:13:44.490
But the controversy swirling around Eleanor's role

01:13:44.490 --> 01:13:47.070
as first lady did not damage FDR,

01:13:53.340 --> 01:13:54.860
won by a landslide.

01:14:06.240 --> 01:14:09.600
The Roosevelts had become extraordinary political partners.

01:14:10.430 --> 01:14:15.910
She kept at him on issues which he might have, in the rush of business, wanted to overlook.

01:14:16.420 --> 01:14:18.540
She kept him to a high standard.

01:14:19.480 --> 01:14:23.180
Anybody who ever saw her lock eyes with him and say,

01:14:23.320 --> 01:14:26.490
now, Franklin, you should, never forgot it.

01:14:26.820 --> 01:14:29.840
And even though he thought her unrealistic sometimes.

01:14:31.180 --> 01:14:37.580
He never lost his affection or his wish to do what he should do because she wanted him to do it.

01:14:38.700 --> 01:14:47.140
Very often, he would bait me into giving an opinion by stating as his own a point of view with which he knew I would disagree.

01:14:47.930 --> 01:14:52.370
I remember one occasion, I became extremely vehement and irritated.

01:14:52.980 --> 01:14:59.900
The next day, to my complete surprise, he calmly stated as his own the arguments that I had given him.

01:15:01.340 --> 01:15:05.420
I think they played a game on this thing that she would stay in her own position.

01:15:05.900 --> 01:15:10.160
And if it got shot down and Roosevelt was criticized,

01:15:10.160 --> 01:15:14.390
he would just turn around and say, oh, you know my missus.

01:15:15.390 --> 01:15:19.230
In other words, FDI used Eleanor to test the limits.

01:15:21.410 --> 01:15:26.830
One instance, I think, where she was testing limits was in the Marian Anderson affair.

01:15:32.140 --> 01:15:43.760
In 1939, the African-American singer Marian Anderson was denied permission to perform in Constitution Hall in Washington, D.C. by the Daughters of the American Revolution.

01:15:45.460 --> 01:15:49.120
Eleanor, whose family had fought in the Revolution, belonged to the DAR.

01:15:51.050 --> 01:15:53.430
In protest, she resigned her membership.

01:15:54.740 --> 01:15:59.040
By then, she had the self-confidence and the strength to stand alone.

01:16:00.160 --> 01:16:07.290
because she knew in the depths of her soul that this was wrong.

01:16:09.040 --> 01:16:11.380
Eleanor worked quietly behind the scenes.

01:16:12.470 --> 01:16:15.710
She helped arrange for Anderson to sing at the Lincoln Memorial.

01:16:16.370 --> 01:16:21.500
75,000 mass before Lincoln Memorial to hear Marian Anderson, colored contralto,

01:16:22.080 --> 01:16:24.720
make her capital debut at the Great Emancipator Shrine.

01:16:43.310 --> 01:16:51.290
My Country Tis of Thee, her first song, she put such great emphasis upon liberty.

01:16:52.240 --> 01:16:57.560
The D.A.R.'s refusal to allow her to sing was a breach of that liberty.

01:16:58.120 --> 01:16:59.640
Sweet love.

01:17:08.100 --> 01:17:11.560
And there were tears in my eyes.

01:17:11.680 --> 01:17:16.400
I think there were tears in the eyes of almost everybody in that huge crowd.

01:17:21.980 --> 01:17:27.440
I'm not too sure that America realized what that concert symbolized.

01:17:31.630 --> 01:17:35.450
because it struck at the very depths of racism in America.

01:17:36.810 --> 01:17:40.230
And everybody knew that Mrs. Roosevelt was behind this.

01:17:40.980 --> 01:17:47.990
This was something unique, having a first lady in the White House who was a good friend.

01:17:47.990 --> 01:17:51.840
She was much more of a friend than Franklin.

01:17:52.390 --> 01:17:59.970
Franklin was a politician, and he weighed the political consequences of...

01:18:00.220 --> 01:18:03.440
every answer and every step that he took.

01:18:04.040 --> 01:18:05.680
He was a good politician, too.

01:18:06.450 --> 01:18:09.210
But she spoke out of her conscience

01:18:09.210 --> 01:18:13.820
and acted as a conscientious person.

01:18:15.290 --> 01:18:16.350
That was different.

01:18:53.260 --> 01:18:59.350
That Easter Sunday in 1939, the world was just months away from the start of war in Europe.

01:19:01.820 --> 01:19:10.800
The New Deal programs that Eleanor Roosevelt had worked so hard to foster were already being cut back as her husband struggled to arm America.

01:19:13.730 --> 01:19:23.950
In the turbulent times ahead, Eleanor would be bitterly attacked for what she believed in, and once again, she would face loss and betrayal.

01:19:46.730 --> 01:19:51.230
Now the long-awaited hour, the nominations, the Honorable Lister Hill, junior senator from Alabama.

01:19:51.250 --> 01:19:59.970
On July 17th, 1940, Eleanor Roosevelt was at her home, Val Kill, listening to the radio broadcast of the Democratic National.

01:20:00.000 --> 01:20:01.560
at the National Convention in Chicago.

01:20:02.300 --> 01:20:03.680
BEDLAM BEGINS!

01:20:07.440 --> 01:20:14.240
With the world at war in Europe and Asia,

01:20:14.440 --> 01:20:16.740
the delegates had nominated Franklin Roosevelt

01:20:16.740 --> 01:20:20.080
to run for an unprecedented third term as president.

01:20:20.380 --> 01:20:22.000
Franklin Roosevelt!

01:20:32.040 --> 01:20:38.580
Now he sent word that he wanted the controversial Secretary of Agriculture, Henry Wallace, as his running mate.

01:20:40.680 --> 01:20:42.140
The delegates threatened to revolt.

01:20:44.890 --> 01:20:47.850
From the White House, Roosevelt threatened not to run.

01:20:50.360 --> 01:20:52.540
The convention was spinning out of control.

01:20:55.190 --> 01:20:59.970
Party leaders and an anxious FDR turned to his strongest ally to help.

01:21:00.000 --> 01:21:07.680
hold the convention together I got a call from the convention begging me to

01:21:07.680 --> 01:21:15.270
come out I called Franklin and he said well would you like to go and I said no

01:21:15.270 --> 01:21:20.080
I wouldn't like to go I'm very busy and you told me I didn't have to go he said

01:21:20.320 --> 01:21:28.290
well perhaps they seem to think it might be well if you came out and I said but

01:21:28.290 --> 01:21:29.930
do you really want

01:21:30.840 --> 01:21:35.500
And he said, well, perhaps it would be a good idea.

01:21:36.920 --> 01:21:40.440
And so that meant, I suppose, that I had to go.

01:22:00.000 --> 01:22:01.920
had broken loose in the hall.

01:22:02.120 --> 01:22:03.840
You couldn't hear yourself speak.

01:22:04.960 --> 01:22:06.800
The noise was something terrible.

01:22:07.730 --> 01:22:14.170
I went forward and stood,

01:22:17.450 --> 01:22:20.330
and to my surprise, and everybody else's, I imagine,

01:22:21.360 --> 01:22:24.160
there was silence in a very short time.

01:22:44.520 --> 01:22:46.960
She talked very briefly without notes,

01:22:47.180 --> 01:22:50.060
though she had very carefully prepared it.

01:23:01.380 --> 01:23:04.130
It just brought us all up.

01:23:04.310 --> 01:23:07.230
She was electric is about the only thing I can think of.

01:23:33.480 --> 01:23:36.180
There was pandemonium. So much applause.

01:23:40.400 --> 01:23:45.850
Pulled it together. They agreed to the president's choice for vice president.

01:23:47.520 --> 01:23:48.960
And it was a miracle.

01:23:50.210 --> 01:23:51.850
People loved every minute of it.

01:23:53.070 --> 01:23:56.940
Some people cried and some people just, it was just amazing.

01:24:00.320 --> 01:24:06.270
The president called her and said this was a job wonderfully done.

01:24:06.510 --> 01:24:07.850
It was just marvelous.

01:24:08.130 --> 01:24:11.070
And Mrs. Roosevelt sat there and beamed.

01:24:24.780 --> 01:24:29.740
Eleanor exulted in her triumph, but the election itself.

01:24:30.120 --> 01:24:36.730
filled her with dread. More than a quarter century in politics had taken a heavy toll on her family.

01:24:38.690 --> 01:24:44.320
Just before Election Day, Eleanor wrote, from a personal standpoint, I'd give anything to leave

01:24:44.320 --> 01:24:51.100
Washington. And if Franklin is elected, I wonder if the amount he can do will be worth the sacrifice

01:24:51.100 --> 01:24:59.890
that all of us have to make. Publicly, Eleanor did not reveal how much she worried.

01:25:00.000 --> 01:25:00.840
about her family.

01:25:01.990 --> 01:25:04.490
All the Roosevelt children had troubled lives.

01:25:05.270 --> 01:25:07.310
They struggled with feelings of jealousy,

01:25:07.760 --> 01:25:10.720
with failed marriages, and financial difficulties.

01:25:11.400 --> 01:25:15.240
Among the five children, there were 19 marriages.

01:25:17.070 --> 01:25:20.470
Hardly that might be due to the puzzlement

01:25:21.100 --> 01:25:24.650
of being children of famous people

01:25:24.650 --> 01:25:27.110
and not knowing who was your friend, really,

01:25:27.230 --> 01:25:29.970
or who wanted to get close to your parents.

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:38.940
parents. Mrs. Roosevelt tried very hard to make it clear to them that access to their father was

01:25:38.940 --> 01:25:45.590
really often the main purpose of why people fell all over themselves for them. But it was difficult

01:25:45.590 --> 01:25:53.430
for them to believe. White House is a very seductive place to be. It was very difficult

01:25:53.430 --> 01:25:59.980
for them as the children, not just of the president of the United States.

01:26:00.060 --> 01:26:06.730
but of Eleanor Roosevelt as well I mean they sort of had a double whammy they

01:26:06.730 --> 01:26:11.550
wanted to really talk to their father they had to ask for an appointment and

01:26:12.020 --> 01:26:17.130
even when they got the appointment sometimes affairs of state would be so

01:26:17.130 --> 01:26:22.340
important that they didn't get the full attention of their father and this is a

01:26:22.340 --> 01:26:29.750
very difficult thing for youngsters to accept now 56 Eleanor still

01:26:30.000 --> 01:26:38.420
herself for not having been a better mother, feelings of guilt and inadequacy

01:26:38.580 --> 01:26:45.180
continued to send her into fierce depressions. When she had one of her

01:26:45.180 --> 01:26:51.210
Griselda moods, there was practically nothing you could do. I never fully

01:26:51.210 --> 01:26:58.990
understood what brought on this great sudden sadness and withdrawal. She could

01:26:58.990 --> 01:26:59.810
turn to ice.

01:27:00.700 --> 01:27:02.620
I was scared to death of those moods.

01:27:03.490 --> 01:27:09.040
I think that she was a moody, even maybe sad person

01:27:09.040 --> 01:27:16.240
because she felt in herself a lack of ability to be spontaneous.

01:27:17.530 --> 01:27:20.430
I think she wanted to be a happy person.

01:27:24.700 --> 01:27:26.860
Image of her in the White House.

01:27:30.380 --> 01:27:46.700
i was leaving and she came to the door with me and she stood out under the portico and just stood there waving goodbye while we drove down the driveway and i had this terrible feeling of a lonely lonely person

01:28:00.540 --> 01:28:04.700
I'm speaking to you tonight at a very serious moment in our history.

01:28:06.400 --> 01:28:09.740
Army and Navy officials have been with the president all afternoon.

01:28:10.260 --> 01:28:14.300
In fact, the Japanese ambassador was talking to the president

01:28:14.300 --> 01:28:21.140
at the very time that Japan's airships were bombing our citizens in Hawaii and the Philippines.

01:28:21.420 --> 01:28:25.040
The attack on Pearl Harbor that brought America into World War II

01:28:25.040 --> 01:28:28.060
changed Eleanor and Franklin's political partnership.

01:28:30.250 --> 01:28:32.990
FDR's priority now was to win the war,

01:28:33.010 --> 01:28:37.540
and he had less and less time to spend on domestic issues with her.

01:28:39.220 --> 01:28:42.440
As the country mobilized, their four sons enlisted.

01:28:43.740 --> 01:28:45.780
She knew they all would have to go to war.

01:28:47.340 --> 01:28:49.750
The American young people, so many of them,

01:28:49.850 --> 01:28:52.790
she had a feeling would die, and she was deeply disturbed.

01:28:57.290 --> 01:28:59.850
She feared that her sons wouldn't all come back.

01:29:01.120 --> 01:29:06.480
Because the probability was they wouldn't, and it undid her.

01:29:17.450 --> 01:29:20.770
Eleanor still confided many of her feelings to Lorena Hickok,

01:29:21.660 --> 01:29:25.240
but the intensity of their relationship had diminished over the years.

01:29:26.590 --> 01:29:29.970
Now Eleanor often turned for emotional support to a...

01:29:30.000 --> 01:29:37.000
young man the same age as her sons, Joseph Lash. Lash had been a student leader affiliated

01:29:37.000 --> 01:29:43.360
with the American Youth Congress. When a congressional committee investigated foreign influence on

01:29:43.360 --> 01:29:51.770
American political groups, the Youth Congress was targeted. Despite warnings that many

01:29:51.770 --> 01:29:57.110
of its members were communists, Eleanor believed that they were simply idealistic, and she

01:29:57.110 --> 01:29:58.970
defended their right to free speech.

01:30:00.000 --> 01:30:04.920
When I was testifying, Mrs. Roosevelt appeared and the hearings weren't finished and we were

01:30:04.920 --> 01:30:06.280
supposed to come back the next day.

01:30:06.280 --> 01:30:08.060
She said, I can take six of you.

01:30:08.200 --> 01:30:13.900
So she scooped up into her limousine and lo and behold, we were having dinner with the

01:30:13.900 --> 01:30:18.160
president and that night we spent at the White House.

01:30:19.900 --> 01:30:22.380
The next morning she came to the hearings again.

01:30:22.920 --> 01:30:24.380
The hearing room came alive.

01:30:25.200 --> 01:30:26.920
Everybody was on their toes.

01:30:26.920 --> 01:30:29.980
And you have admitted that Ms. Celeste Tracht is a member of the...

01:30:30.000 --> 01:30:32.620
No, Mr. Matthews, that was your statement.

01:30:32.720 --> 01:30:33.360
I agreed with it.

01:30:33.480 --> 01:30:34.160
You agreed with me.

01:30:40.750 --> 01:30:44.020
Somehow sort of hit it off, and from that point on,

01:30:44.160 --> 01:30:47.580
it became, I think for several years, her closest friendship.

01:30:50.110 --> 01:30:53.390
They enjoyed the same kind of jokes, the sense of humor,

01:30:54.000 --> 01:30:59.010
but above all, they belonged to the same part of the human equation,

01:30:59.010 --> 01:30:59.970
and they had...

01:31:00.000 --> 01:31:04.160
the same kind of mission, the same kind of way of looking at the world.

01:31:06.410 --> 01:31:13.310
It was real love on both sides. Joe adored her. I mean, he was someone like nobody had

01:31:13.310 --> 01:31:20.450
ever seen. Certainly it was a friendship between a man and a woman, but there was no sexual

01:31:20.450 --> 01:31:26.510
part to it. And it made both of them very eager to see each other and to talk together.

01:31:27.590 --> 01:31:29.970
Eleanor did not seem to realize their friendship.

01:31:30.000 --> 01:31:31.600
might be misconstrued.

01:31:32.080 --> 01:31:38.080
In 1942, Lash was assigned to an air base in Illinois.

01:31:39.590 --> 01:31:41.210
Because of his radical background,

01:31:41.960 --> 01:31:44.840
military counterintelligence monitored his activities,

01:31:45.620 --> 01:31:48.100
including two weekends he spent with Eleanor,

01:31:48.890 --> 01:31:51.090
one at the Blackstone Hotel in Chicago.

01:31:52.560 --> 01:31:56.030
Jo had a room next to hers and came into her room.

01:31:56.940 --> 01:31:59.560
He was very tired. He had not slept all night.

01:31:59.660 --> 01:31:59.960
And she said,

01:32:00.000 --> 01:32:05.360
why don't you lie down, Joe, and sat on the bed next to him and talked with him.

01:32:05.640 --> 01:32:08.430
And I'm sure that that was all.

01:32:09.730 --> 01:32:12.870
Mrs. Roosevelt is advised by officials of the Blackstone Hotel

01:32:12.870 --> 01:32:16.480
that military intelligence had been monitoring her during her stay.

01:32:17.400 --> 01:32:20.660
She had real difficulties with the surveillance for activities.

01:32:21.680 --> 01:32:23.540
She wanted to preserve her privacy,

01:32:23.540 --> 01:32:26.860
and she wanted to have the freedom to move around as any other American.

01:32:26.900 --> 01:32:29.920
So she complained to the White House.

01:32:30.000 --> 01:32:35.570
aide, Harry Hopkins, who in turn conveyed her displeasure to General of the Army, George

01:32:35.570 --> 01:32:44.060
Marshall. Marshall put a stop to the surveillance, but the agents gave their file with its allegation

01:32:44.060 --> 01:32:56.150
of a sexual affair to FBI Director J. Edgar Hoover. If they had listened to how they talked,

01:32:56.170 --> 01:32:59.390
they would have found out what this relationship was all about.

01:33:00.120 --> 01:33:09.910
close she might have called him joe dearest as she did but this was then the hoover interpretation

01:33:11.500 --> 01:33:16.560
hoover knows everything about what goes on in all the bedrooms in the nation and if he didn't

01:33:16.560 --> 01:33:26.330
know he'd invent it this was not the fbi's first report on eleanor eleanor roosevelt's fbi file

01:33:26.330 --> 01:33:29.270
is one of the wonders of the Western world.

01:33:30.480 --> 01:33:34.520
It is one of the largest individual files that Hoover compiled,

01:33:34.520 --> 01:33:38.340
and it goes on for over 3,000 pages.

01:33:39.750 --> 01:33:42.170
She writes to Joe Ash. The letter's in her file.

01:33:42.350 --> 01:33:45.750
She visits with Joe Ash. The visit is in her file.

01:33:46.790 --> 01:33:51.950
Anything she says against segregation, against lynching, is in that file.

01:33:52.550 --> 01:33:54.530
They are following her everywhere.

01:33:55.640 --> 01:33:58.900
Hoover's notes reveal his intense dislike of Eleanor.

01:34:00.000 --> 01:34:02.900
and the FBI would watch her for the rest of her life.

01:34:03.610 --> 01:34:06.790
It reflected a conviction on the part of senior FBI officials

01:34:06.790 --> 01:34:10.030
that Mrs. Roosevelt's advocacy of racial equality

01:34:10.030 --> 01:34:12.570
was inimical to the national interests.

01:34:13.690 --> 01:34:17.530
He found her a threat to American society and values.

01:34:24.480 --> 01:34:28.380
It's all Negroes, U.S. 92nd Division, 1st College, continuing.

01:34:38.220 --> 01:34:42.120
For Eleanor, the war against fascist Germany and Japan

01:34:42.120 --> 01:34:47.080
made America's own failings, especially racism, even more intolerable.

01:34:48.480 --> 01:34:52.320
Her public support of racial equality enraged many Americans.

01:34:54.380 --> 01:34:58.660
World War II exposed a great contradiction in American life.

01:35:00.090 --> 01:35:05.780
Here you were fighting Hitler, the world's premier ideologue of racism.

01:35:06.980 --> 01:35:15.300
And in your own country, if you were a black soldier in a uniform, you had to be very cautious about your life.

01:35:19.660 --> 01:35:28.590
They were still lynching African Americans, hanging them up, setting them on fire, shooting them like they were garbage and dogs.

01:35:32.720 --> 01:35:40.390
During the war, thousands of people, black and white, moved to northern cities like Detroit to work in defense industries.

01:35:41.290 --> 01:35:47.820
In Detroit, you had a lot of workers there that said, we just can't accept black people.

01:35:49.110 --> 01:35:59.550
We cannot accept black people in any kind of jobs that we had declared rigidly in our minds not to be black jobs.

01:36:05.760 --> 01:36:09.420
By 1942, the atmosphere in Detroit was ugly.

01:36:12.190 --> 01:36:15.010
Blacks and whites clashed in housing projects

01:36:15.010 --> 01:36:16.230
and on street corners.

01:36:19.410 --> 01:36:22.230
Again and again, Eleanor called for tolerance,

01:36:22.610 --> 01:36:25.770
for equal housing and job opportunities for all Americans.

01:36:25.770 --> 01:36:29.890
And she wrote one memo after another, warning

01:36:30.000 --> 01:36:47.340
fdr about rising racial tensions on june 20th 1943 eleanor's worst fears came true riots ripped

01:36:47.340 --> 01:36:57.350
through detroit white mobs roamed the streets hauling blacks out of cars and beating them

01:36:58.040 --> 01:36:59.960
African Americans responded in

01:37:00.060 --> 01:37:07.110
The next morning, federal troops arrived in restored order.

01:37:17.440 --> 01:37:23.140
Nearly 1,000 people were injured, 25 blacks, and 9 whites were dead.

01:37:26.730 --> 01:37:29.250
As America tried to understand what had happened,

01:37:30.000 --> 01:37:37.560
newspapers found an easy scapegoat. And of course, who did they blame? Eleanor Roosevelt and they

01:37:37.560 --> 01:37:44.140
laid it on her. They're accused of being a communist. They're accused of everything. I asked

01:37:44.140 --> 01:37:50.240
Mrs. Roosevelt one day, I said, why do you do so many things that make you so controversial? She

01:37:50.240 --> 01:37:57.930
said, I have access to the president. And if I don't use that access to do things that need to

01:37:57.930 --> 01:37:59.850
be done in this country, it need to be done.

01:38:00.000 --> 01:38:04.300
people, I would be sorely remiss and irresponsible.

01:38:06.230 --> 01:38:12.250
In the weeks following Detroit, Eleanor and civil rights leaders appealed to FDR to speak

01:38:12.250 --> 01:38:14.310
to the nation about the problem of race.

01:38:15.270 --> 01:38:20.770
But Roosevelt felt his hands were tied by powerful white Southerners in Congress, whose

01:38:20.770 --> 01:38:22.530
votes he needed for the war effort.

01:38:23.410 --> 01:38:25.250
He had a war to win, he said.

01:38:25.250 --> 01:38:29.980
The ultimate objectives in this war continue to be...

01:38:31.450 --> 01:38:40.620
The campaign in the Pacific was one of the toughest and bloodiest of the war.

01:38:42.290 --> 01:38:45.510
For months, Eleanor had wanted to visit the troops fighting there.

01:38:47.560 --> 01:38:52.310
Now FDR agreed, thinking the trip might reduce her negative press.

01:38:54.550 --> 01:38:59.440
In August 1943, she set off alone, without even her secretary.

01:39:01.400 --> 01:39:02.960
She went with trepidation.

01:39:03.420 --> 01:39:07.120
She knew that vicious Eleanor stories were common among the men.

01:39:07.960 --> 01:39:10.790
Word had got out that a woman was arriving.

01:39:10.790 --> 01:39:15.180
For security reasons, they couldn't say it was the president's wife.

01:39:15.660 --> 01:39:19.120
And she said she always felt how disappointed they would be

01:39:19.120 --> 01:39:22.940
because they were expecting a Hollywood starlet and all they got was her.

01:39:24.420 --> 01:39:27.620
On the advice of her sons, who were all serving in the military,

01:39:27.960 --> 01:39:29.800
she insisted on spending time...

01:39:30.000 --> 01:39:33.000
not just with the officers, but with the enlisted men.

01:39:33.480 --> 01:39:37.020
Just as the Marines were ordered to leave Guadalcanal,

01:39:37.430 --> 01:39:43.830
an officer found a pilot feeling very sad, looking very depressed.

01:39:43.990 --> 01:39:45.430
And he said, what's the matter with you?

01:39:45.980 --> 01:39:49.800
And he said, oh, I just can't go home.

01:39:49.800 --> 01:39:51.560
I haven't shot a jack.

01:39:52.160 --> 01:39:57.180
And so the officer said, well, listen, I'll tell you what to do.

01:39:57.260 --> 01:39:59.800
Will you go up to that ridge over there and jump?

01:40:00.410 --> 01:40:06.670
of a sudden and say the hell with your agito and they'll jump up other people all around and if you

01:40:06.670 --> 01:40:15.900
shoot those two get a jab so he came by a while later and uh green was still looking very do me and

01:40:15.900 --> 01:40:24.500
he said did you do what i told you to do and um yes sir yes i ran up there and i did just

01:40:24.500 --> 01:40:28.780
what you told me to do and i said to hell with your agito and they jumped up just what you told me

01:40:28.780 --> 01:40:29.140
They're good.

01:40:49.360 --> 01:40:50.320
The men loved her.

01:40:53.980 --> 01:40:58.980
Admiral Halsey, commander of the Pacific Fleet, had opposed her visit, but he changed his

01:40:58.980 --> 01:40:59.320
mind.

01:41:01.560 --> 01:41:04.820
I marveled at her hardyhood, both physical and mental, he reported.

01:41:06.230 --> 01:41:08.670
She saw patients who were grievously wounded.

01:41:10.080 --> 01:41:13.240
I marveled most at their expressions as she leaned over them.

01:41:14.340 --> 01:41:15.940
It was a sight I will never forget.

01:41:18.420 --> 01:41:22.640
She alone accomplished more good than any other person who passed through my area.

01:41:28.520 --> 01:41:29.700
The suffering of the men.

01:41:30.000 --> 01:41:34.070
Eleanor said, left a mark from which I think I shall never be free.

01:41:41.290 --> 01:41:44.950
By 1944, the war had taken its toll on FDR.

01:41:46.040 --> 01:41:49.560
62 years old, he had been president for 11 years.

01:41:50.520 --> 01:41:52.360
Now he sought a fourth term.

01:41:53.870 --> 01:41:56.630
Eleanor Roosevelt felt that he desperately needed rest.

01:41:57.730 --> 01:41:59.970
On the other hand, she did not feel...

01:42:00.000 --> 01:42:03.940
that she could say or push very hard against his running

01:42:04.480 --> 01:42:06.080
because the war was on,

01:42:06.720 --> 01:42:09.500
and he felt he had to finish the job.

01:42:11.280 --> 01:42:13.060
As I stand here today,

01:42:14.570 --> 01:42:16.830
having taken the solemn oath of office,

01:42:17.770 --> 01:42:20.170
in the presence of my fellow countrymen...

01:42:20.740 --> 01:42:23.040
The grandchildren came back for the inauguration.

01:42:24.160 --> 01:42:26.340
There were 13 or 14 of us,

01:42:26.340 --> 01:42:29.660
and we brought our homework with us and stayed a month.

01:42:30.000 --> 01:42:35.980
the White House. And then when I had to go back to school in San Francisco, I popped into the Oval

01:42:35.980 --> 01:42:42.760
Office and said goodbye. And I said, I'll see you this summer, Papa. And he said, yes, goodbye,

01:42:42.900 --> 01:42:51.760
old thing. But I noticed how thin he looked. He didn't quite fill out his clothes. And I wondered

01:42:51.760 --> 01:42:59.760
how long he can go on. Franklin Roosevelt was ill and suffering from heart disease. And

01:43:00.000 --> 01:43:03.360
Eleanor Roosevelt seemed oddly oblivious of this,

01:43:04.020 --> 01:43:07.050
and she would nag him to do things,

01:43:07.400 --> 01:43:10.900
helping a particular group of refugees, that sort of thing,

01:43:11.100 --> 01:43:14.570
when he was trying to, A, stay alive,

01:43:14.860 --> 01:43:18.740
and B, keep the big picture in his mind.

01:43:19.650 --> 01:43:23.710
Very rarely did she bring in papers to the cocktail hour

01:43:23.710 --> 01:43:24.950
because that was his hour.

01:43:25.390 --> 01:43:29.860
But in one instance, my grandmother did bring a lot of papers in.

01:43:30.160 --> 01:43:32.320
And he must have been very, very tired.

01:43:32.820 --> 01:43:37.870
And so he picked up the papers and he flopped them down in front of Anna, my mother,

01:43:38.090 --> 01:43:40.450
and said, sis, you handle these.

01:43:40.910 --> 01:43:44.450
And it was totally out of character for him.

01:43:46.120 --> 01:43:51.460
So my grandmother just left in hurt and went back to her study.

01:43:51.740 --> 01:43:57.910
And my mother did say to her father, pa, I think you've got to make amends.

01:43:58.580 --> 01:43:59.860
And he said, yes, I know.

01:44:01.052 --> 01:44:03.532
So he had himself wheeled in and no one knows what.

01:44:03.872 --> 01:44:04.872
He said.

01:44:05.084 --> 01:44:06.104
But she came down.

01:44:06.428 --> 01:44:06.928
to supper.

01:44:07.324 --> 01:44:08.324
with him.

01:44:11.484 --> 01:44:14.604
FDR depended more and more on their daughter, Anna.

01:44:17.180 --> 01:44:18.340
Unlike her mother,

01:44:18.492 --> 01:44:20.492
Anna made no demands on her father.

01:44:22.812 --> 01:44:24.092
She was warm and cheerful.

01:44:24.508 --> 01:44:26.508
and she carefully watched his health.

01:44:31.372 --> 01:44:33.132
To Eleanor's great disappointment,

01:44:33.420 --> 01:44:35.260
He asked Anna, not her.

01:44:35.660 --> 01:44:37.500
to accompany him to Yalta.

01:44:37.580 --> 01:44:40.340
for his meeting with Joseph Stalin and Winston Churchill.

01:44:48.524 --> 01:44:49.024
Congress.

01:44:49.836 --> 01:44:51.236
when FDR returned.

01:44:51.436 --> 01:44:53.656
His failing health was apparent to all.

01:44:54.124 --> 01:44:56.124
I hope that you will pardon me for...

01:44:56.844 --> 01:44:59.344
an unusual posture of sitting down.

01:45:00.190 --> 01:45:02.210
during the presentation of what I want to say,

01:45:02.350 --> 01:45:05.850
I know that you will realize that it makes it a lot easier for me.

01:45:06.070 --> 01:45:12.230
When Franklin went to Warm Springs in April of 1945,

01:45:13.050 --> 01:45:14.850
he said to me,

01:45:15.000 --> 01:45:18.480
that he felt that there were certain things I had to do

01:45:18.480 --> 01:45:20.540
and I had better wait and come down later,

01:45:21.180 --> 01:45:25.280
he would take two people whom he enjoyed having with him,

01:45:25.970 --> 01:45:28.970
Margaret Suclay and Laura Delano,

01:45:29.270 --> 01:45:29.970
and...

01:45:30.000 --> 01:45:34.880
and he said in an amusing way that he did not have to make any effort with either of them.

01:45:36.780 --> 01:45:38.780
Eleanor stayed behind in Washington.

01:45:40.876 --> 01:45:45.116
On April 12th, she attended a fundraising event at the Salgrave Club.

01:45:46.572 --> 01:45:47.932
just before 5 o'clock.

01:45:48.204 --> 01:45:51.304
She received a telephone call telling her to come home immediately.

01:45:53.356 --> 01:45:57.296
I got into the car and sat with clenched hands all the way to the White House.

01:46:09.436 --> 01:46:10.436
In my heart of hearts,

01:46:10.656 --> 01:46:11.896
I knew what had happened.

01:46:18.268 --> 01:46:21.228
Franklin had died that afternoon of a brain hemorrhage.

01:46:26.972 --> 01:46:29.312
Eleanor traveled through the night to Warm Springs.

01:46:30.190 --> 01:46:33.290
And there she learned why he had discouraged her from going with him.

01:46:36.260 --> 01:46:44.650
Laura Delano made it her business to tell Eleanor that Lucy Mercer was with FDR when he died in Warm Springs.

01:46:45.000 --> 01:46:52.910
which was devastating for Eleanor to learn that the woman FDR had first had

01:46:52.910 --> 01:46:57.940
an affair with in 1917 was there at his side when he died.

01:47:02.428 --> 01:47:04.968
And she discovers also that her daughter, Anna,

01:47:05.436 --> 01:47:12.316
had arranged for many other visits during the war years between Lucy Mercer and her husband.

01:47:13.436 --> 01:47:15.196
And this is a very big blow.

01:47:15.740 --> 01:47:17.100
to her pride.

01:47:17.308 --> 01:47:18.068
to her heart.

01:47:26.236 --> 01:47:29.476
as Franklin's body was carried on the journey to Washington.

01:47:30.028 --> 01:47:32.028
Eleanor was barely seen.

01:48:08.380 --> 01:48:11.560
For more than 40 years, for better and for worse,

01:48:12.160 --> 01:48:14.620
Eleanor's life had been intertwined with Franklin's.

01:48:17.500 --> 01:48:20.720
Now in her grief and her anger, she felt numb,

01:48:21.180 --> 01:48:23.000
detached from the nation's sorrow.

01:48:37.930 --> 01:48:44.950
It was almost as though I had erected someone a little outside of myself who was the president's wife, she wrote.

01:48:46.110 --> 01:48:49.610
I was lost somewhere deep down inside my cell.

01:49:01.080 --> 01:49:07.400
Just days after FDR's funeral, Eleanor moved out of the White House back to her home, Val Kill.

01:49:11.420 --> 01:49:18.520
She had time to reflect on Franklin's death, his final betrayal, and Anna's complicity.

01:49:20.110 --> 01:49:23.250
Her heart has been really shredded.

01:49:24.120 --> 01:49:29.570
It took a long time for her to forgive her daughter, and she does forgive her.

01:49:30.000 --> 01:49:32.180
And she even understands how it happened.

01:49:33.750 --> 01:49:37.950
As she struggled with her feelings, the depth of her mourning surprised her.

01:49:39.980 --> 01:49:49.000
My husband and I had come through the years with an acceptance of each other's faults and foibles, warm affection and agreement on essential values.

01:49:49.000 --> 01:49:51.720
We depended on each other.

01:49:53.160 --> 01:49:58.320
She was much more intimately connected with the president than she thought.

01:49:59.300 --> 01:49:59.940
She took it.

01:50:00.000 --> 01:50:06.200
granted that she could go to the Oval Office and ask him questions that he knew the answers to

01:50:06.200 --> 01:50:12.280
and nobody else did. She felt that the warmth of going to the president's bedroom in the morning

01:50:12.280 --> 01:50:19.160
and of talking with him, of joking with him at the dinner table, all that had been there and

01:50:19.160 --> 01:50:25.160
And not questioned, and not particularly acknowledged, but now it wasn't there.

01:50:30.060 --> 01:50:41.040
In May 1945, just one month after Franklin's death, Germany surrendered to the Allies.

01:50:42.460 --> 01:50:45.390
By August, World War II was finally over.

01:50:47.440 --> 01:50:50.960
Eleanor was relieved, but she did not feel like celebrating.

01:50:52.330 --> 01:50:56.200
I miss Pa's voice, she wrote Anna, and the words he would have spoken.

01:51:03.520 --> 01:51:06.820
Franklin and Eleanor had experienced two world wars.

01:51:07.890 --> 01:51:10.390
They had witnessed unimaginable destruction

01:51:10.390 --> 01:51:13.140
and millions of senseless deaths.

01:51:16.710 --> 01:51:20.150
For years, they had talked about how to prevent another war,

01:51:20.520 --> 01:51:24.200
and FDR had laid the groundwork for the United Nations.

01:51:25.410 --> 01:51:29.860
Give us the wisdom to carry on in the way.

01:51:31.280 --> 01:51:38.620
In December 1945, the new president, Harry Truman, asked Eleanor to be a delegate to

01:51:38.620 --> 01:51:40.660
the UN's first meeting in London.

01:51:48.510 --> 01:51:53.820
Eight months after Franklin's death, Eleanor arrived in England to begin a new career.

01:51:55.620 --> 01:51:59.980
She was a very impressive figure, someone that people queued up.

01:52:00.000 --> 01:52:07.400
to see. Of course, in England, Franklin Roosevelt was a great hero. We thought he was wonderful,

01:52:07.920 --> 01:52:15.250
the way he had come to the help of Britain, and Eleanor came as his widow at that time.

01:52:15.270 --> 01:52:24.150
She was not completely playing her own role. I knew that as the only woman, I'd better be better

01:52:24.150 --> 01:52:29.480
than anybody else. So I read every paper, and they were very dull sometimes.

01:52:30.000 --> 01:52:33.720
because State Department papers can be buried out.

01:52:34.150 --> 01:52:36.740
And I used to almost go to sleep over them.

01:52:37.340 --> 01:52:39.390
But I did read them all.

01:52:40.320 --> 01:52:43.660
I knew that if I in any way failed,

01:52:44.420 --> 01:52:46.080
it would not be just my failure.

01:52:46.220 --> 01:52:47.660
It should be the failure of all women.

01:52:47.840 --> 01:52:49.760
There'd never be another woman on the delegation.

01:52:52.770 --> 01:52:56.440
They signed her to one of the committees,

01:52:56.720 --> 01:52:58.720
a social, humanitarian, and cultural,

01:52:59.420 --> 01:52:59.940
because...

01:53:00.000 --> 01:53:02.220
They didn't think she could do much harm there.

01:53:02.860 --> 01:53:08.180
The condescension of the other male delegates was fairly obvious

01:53:08.270 --> 01:53:13.320
because they didn't know of the Eleanor Roosevelt, who was a political pro.

01:53:14.690 --> 01:53:19.690
The United Nations quickly became the diplomatic battleground of the new Cold War

01:53:19.690 --> 01:53:21.970
between the Soviet Union and the West.

01:53:25.430 --> 01:53:29.970
Eleanor's tough sparring with her Russian counterpart became the talk of the Asset.

01:53:31.660 --> 01:53:39.060
She countered sometimes very violent attacks with firmness and diplomatic politeness,

01:53:39.270 --> 01:53:42.890
but nobody was under any misapprehension of where she stood.

01:53:43.180 --> 01:53:48.940
I'm extremely sorry, but we have to take up your time to go in again...

01:53:57.360 --> 01:53:59.960
When she got into the brickbats and...

01:54:00.060 --> 01:54:05.880
infighting in the U.N. committees and so forth, Eleanor Roosevelt knew how to handle it far better

01:54:05.880 --> 01:54:11.400
than some of these chaps did. The delegates were so impressed with Eleanor's performance,

01:54:11.750 --> 01:54:17.040
they elected her to chair the committee drafting a universal declaration of human rights.

01:54:17.240 --> 01:54:29.980
There are many parts of the world that had not even the elementary understanding of what human rights really...

01:54:30.000 --> 01:54:37.180
meant. And there was a feeling that one needed to define more clearly what human rights and

01:54:37.180 --> 01:54:45.770
freedoms were to mean. It took a year of relentless negotiating. The Soviet Amendment of Article 22

01:54:46.150 --> 01:54:53.290
introduces new elements into the article. All of the naivete that sometimes people attribute

01:54:53.290 --> 01:54:59.940
to her in the 1930s or even 1940s had passed away. An identical text was rejected.

01:55:01.510 --> 01:55:05.410
And she was able to see what's possible.

01:55:05.610 --> 01:55:07.010
How far can I push this?

01:55:07.150 --> 01:55:08.330
How can I get that?

01:55:08.490 --> 01:55:11.330
How can I keep from losing something over here?

01:55:11.570 --> 01:55:17.260
The section of discrimination is comprehensively covered in Article 2 of the Declaration.

01:55:17.780 --> 01:55:21.470
Every comma was argued over in all the languages.

01:55:21.790 --> 01:55:26.990
There were big items and there were small items and points of drafting and points of substance.

01:55:27.410 --> 01:55:29.830
And Eleanor had to rule on many of these.

01:55:30.160 --> 01:55:36.640
it is my ruling as chairman of the commission that the point raised by the soviet member is

01:55:36.640 --> 01:55:45.060
out of order she also enjoyed the work she worked 18 20 hours a day that means she got four to six

01:55:45.060 --> 01:55:51.820
hours sleep a night if that we would be having breakfast and she would say please try and get

01:55:51.820 --> 01:55:58.780
the pakistani madame begum over for supper this evening and see if you can't get mr malik to join

01:55:58.780 --> 01:55:59.920
us and it'd be

01:56:00.060 --> 01:56:06.700
revolving in her head the maneuvering it is not a treaty it is not an international agreement

01:56:06.700 --> 01:56:12.980
it is not and does not purpose to be a statement of law or of legal obligation

01:56:12.980 --> 01:56:21.590
it is a declaration of basic principles of human rights and freedom all human beings are born free

01:56:21.590 --> 01:56:27.420
and equal in dignity and rights they are endowed with reason and conscience and should act towards

01:56:27.420 --> 01:56:29.580
one another in the spirit of brotherhood.

01:56:30.160 --> 01:56:33.640
Well, it started out as all men, which immediately caused a problem.

01:56:34.550 --> 01:56:39.770
Born free and equal in rights, being endowed with reason was something the Soviet bloc

01:56:39.770 --> 01:56:43.640
and those who don't particularly believe in religion fought over.

01:56:44.500 --> 01:56:48.610
And then act towards each other in a spirit of brotherhood.

01:56:48.730 --> 01:56:53.840
Well, the brotherhood stayed because it became rather pompous if you added sisterhood.

01:56:54.550 --> 01:56:57.630
But all men were certainly changed to all human beings.

01:57:00.540 --> 01:57:08.660
consideration of the declaration requires no comment. We are all agreed, I'm sure, that the

01:57:08.660 --> 01:57:24.400
declaration must be approved by this assembly at this session. United States, yes. On December 10th,

01:57:24.500 --> 01:57:29.460
1948, the United Nations finally voted on the document. United Kingdom.

01:57:30.280 --> 01:57:35.580
yes it was adopted at something like three o'clock in the morning in favor of adoption

01:57:36.580 --> 01:57:46.800
48 everyone really felt this was a great historic moment for the world particularly fitting that

01:57:47.210 --> 01:57:54.480
here tonight should be the person who's been the leader in this movement person who was

01:57:54.860 --> 01:57:59.940
raised to even greater honor so great a name i refer of course to mrs roosevelt the del

01:58:00.000 --> 01:58:01.080
of the United States.

01:58:01.240 --> 01:58:08.840
She was becoming a statesperson.

01:58:09.220 --> 01:58:13.400
She'd been mostly concerned with domestic affairs

01:58:13.970 --> 01:58:16.490
while the president was the president of the United States.

01:58:17.000 --> 01:58:20.280
But she undertook this and made her own mark.

01:58:21.330 --> 01:58:25.300
This, in a way, was the making of Eleanor Roosevelt,

01:58:26.030 --> 01:58:29.970
a separate person standing tall on her own two feet.

01:58:30.000 --> 01:58:49.580
After Republican Dwight Eisenhower became president in 1952, Eleanor Roosevelt, like all presidential appointees, resigned her post.

01:58:51.040 --> 01:58:58.350
She was 68. For the first time in nearly 25 years, she had no official duties to perform.

01:59:03.000 --> 01:59:04.340
She traveled extensively.

01:59:04.440 --> 01:59:08.680
She made the first of three trips

01:59:08.680 --> 01:59:11.000
to the newly established state of Israel.

01:59:14.160 --> 01:59:18.280
She visited Japan, the Soviet Union, and India.

01:59:20.200 --> 01:59:21.700
Although her visits were private,

01:59:22.330 --> 01:59:24.150
she was greeted like a head of state

01:59:24.150 --> 01:59:25.550
nearly everywhere she went.

01:59:27.700 --> 01:59:29.980
She was often accompanied by her friend and

01:59:30.000 --> 01:59:38.390
physician, Dr. David Gurevich. Gurevich was the son of Russian emigres, a

01:59:38.390 --> 01:59:45.680
worldly cultivated man. David was a man who loved intimacy and she could

01:59:45.680 --> 01:59:51.070
unburden herself. His being a doctor helped because he was accustomed to

01:59:51.070 --> 01:59:57.720
listen and to advise. So that was very good for her. David was charming. He

01:59:58.670 --> 01:59:59.970
was very courtly.

02:00:00.000 --> 02:00:07.590
a gentle person and he was capable of having fun he could practically be a

02:00:07.590 --> 02:00:13.080
naughty boy and my grandmother would giggle I think Eleanor Roosevelt really

02:00:13.080 --> 02:00:18.230
was in love with David Gurevich and I think she really deeply regretted that

02:00:18.230 --> 02:00:23.220
she was so much older than he and that he had other interests in younger women

02:00:24.080 --> 02:00:28.060
she wanted all of David the language and emotions ran very high

02:00:30.670 --> 02:00:36.360
David, dear, I am not stupid and know that 20 years lie between you and me.

02:00:37.640 --> 02:00:42.980
I know you love youth and beauty and independence, and I would not want to keep you from those

02:00:42.980 --> 02:00:49.960
joys, but I would be so happy and so grateful if there were ways when you wanted me to do

02:00:49.960 --> 02:00:50.880
something for you.

02:00:52.400 --> 02:00:57.080
What I have in the few years I have left is yours before it is anyone else's.

02:00:58.040 --> 02:00:59.480
My whole heart is yours.

02:01:01.920 --> 02:01:03.500
She was realistic enough in the end.

02:01:03.720 --> 02:01:07.660
And when he married, she rallied heroically

02:01:07.660 --> 02:01:10.940
and remained a companion and a good friend.

02:01:18.520 --> 02:01:20.940
Elena found a measure of personal happiness,

02:01:20.940 --> 02:01:24.450
but she was still troubled by her failure as a mother.

02:01:25.580 --> 02:01:29.960
She was very concerned about her sons because they...

02:01:30.000 --> 02:01:36.420
into so many ventures and so many of them didn't succeed and with franklin she had the feeling that

02:01:36.420 --> 02:01:44.970
he was relying too much on his charm and didn't work hard enough franklin was probably uh the

02:01:46.120 --> 02:01:52.840
most political of her children looked just like his father talked like his father sounded like his

02:01:52.840 --> 02:01:55.610
father. He was very, very charismatic.

02:02:00.000 --> 02:02:05.760
When FDR Jr. was elected to Congress in 1949, it seemed he had a golden future.

02:02:06.780 --> 02:02:10.240
But like most of his siblings, he lacked his parents' discipline.

02:02:11.240 --> 02:02:15.380
If you're elected to Congress, one assumes you're going to spend some time there.

02:02:16.550 --> 02:02:17.650
Franklin spent very little.

02:02:18.640 --> 02:02:20.970
He preferred the high life in New York.

02:02:21.090 --> 02:02:24.030
He was a young man, and he was spirited and attractive.

02:02:24.770 --> 02:02:26.430
New York was his meat.

02:02:26.950 --> 02:02:27.930
He just loved it.

02:02:28.350 --> 02:02:29.950
He was a...

02:02:29.950 --> 02:02:29.970
Thank you.

02:02:30.090 --> 02:02:38.900
Yes, he was a party animal, like other people, and he enjoyed playing around, whatever.

02:02:39.660 --> 02:02:42.800
He had five wives. That speaks for itself, I guess.

02:02:44.300 --> 02:02:49.480
After his term in Congress, he tried, like his father before him, to run for governor of New York.

02:02:50.310 --> 02:02:52.830
But he lost the nomination to Averill Harriman.

02:02:54.180 --> 02:02:58.540
He ran for attorney general instead. Again, he lost.

02:03:00.000 --> 02:03:07.760
after the election. I was in a hotel where Frank was staying, and he suddenly, I heard him weeping

02:03:07.760 --> 02:03:14.680
in the bathroom, weeping openly, and this was totally out of character. He came out, his eyes

02:03:14.680 --> 02:03:20.750
were red, and I said, Frank, what in the world's trouble? Are you all right? He shook his head and

02:03:20.750 --> 02:03:28.160
said to me, Lou, I must tell you, he said, it's just too much, just too much for one individual

02:03:28.160 --> 02:03:29.980
to bear. And I said, what do you mean? He said,

02:03:30.000 --> 02:03:34.410
to be the son of Franklin D. Roosevelt and Eleanor Roosevelt.

02:03:34.610 --> 02:03:39.810
He said, it's so much to live up to, and I guess I've not done it.

02:03:41.340 --> 02:03:45.060
The Roosevelt children often resented that their mother cared so deeply

02:03:45.060 --> 02:03:48.730
for the needs of her friends and of complete strangers.

02:03:49.750 --> 02:03:54.050
My mother and uncles felt my grandmother had not given to them

02:03:54.050 --> 02:03:58.780
that which she was capable of giving to people outside.

02:04:00.090 --> 02:04:03.910
They saw Joe Lash having, as we would say today,

02:04:04.090 --> 02:04:05.510
a quality relationship.

02:04:05.750 --> 02:04:08.720
That was something they never experienced.

02:04:09.750 --> 02:04:13.370
So there was no small amount of jealousy.

02:04:13.670 --> 02:04:15.010
It was quite, quite plain.

02:04:15.350 --> 02:04:16.690
I understood that always.

02:04:16.950 --> 02:04:17.870
I would have been too.

02:04:19.140 --> 02:04:21.680
They felt they weren't the most important ones.

02:04:22.820 --> 02:04:23.880
Though I don't believe that.

02:04:23.960 --> 02:04:24.640
I think they were.

02:04:24.820 --> 02:04:26.820
But they weren't the exclusive ones.

02:04:28.000 --> 02:04:29.960
There were many others of us who worked.

02:04:30.000 --> 02:04:45.120
Eleanor still pushed herself with a relentless schedule of lectures and meetings, travel and committees.

02:04:45.580 --> 02:04:50.080
She denounced Senator Joseph McCarthy's anti-communist witch hunt.

02:04:51.110 --> 02:04:55.710
She was on the board of the National Association for the Advancement of Colored People,

02:04:56.110 --> 02:04:59.970
and she spoke to audiences around the country trying to build...

02:05:00.000 --> 02:05:02.000
held public support for desegregation.

02:05:06.110 --> 02:05:12.810
Eleanor Roosevelt really wants a Democrat back in office, and she really wants her convictions

02:05:12.810 --> 02:05:15.190
back on the national agenda.

02:05:15.350 --> 02:05:19.060
And she does everything to make that happen.

02:05:19.260 --> 02:05:28.990
In 1952, and again in 1956, Eleanor's choice for president was the former governor of Illinois,

02:05:29.010 --> 02:05:29.970
Adley Steele.

02:05:30.000 --> 02:05:33.780
I'm confident that I'm going to carry the primary and floor tomorrow.

02:05:34.200 --> 02:05:36.540
I think he would make the best president.

02:05:37.050 --> 02:05:43.920
She believed he was an idealistic man, a man of great vision, who could motivate large

02:05:43.920 --> 02:05:47.720
numbers of people to make a more humane and just society.

02:05:49.520 --> 02:05:51.200
She was firm in her opinion.

02:05:51.530 --> 02:05:55.530
He would make the best president of the United States that we had had since Sir Franklin.

02:05:57.980 --> 02:05:59.860
Eleanor was an invaluable ally.

02:06:00.000 --> 02:06:06.880
for Stevenson. She used her prestige to further his candidacy and offered advice gained from

02:06:06.880 --> 02:06:08.960
decades of successful campaigning.

02:06:09.080 --> 02:06:16.260
The first lady of the world, Mrs. Franklin Delano Roosevelt.

02:06:16.540 --> 02:06:29.970
When the Democratic Party convention opened in August 1956, Eleanor was

02:06:30.000 --> 02:06:32.260
asked to address the delegates on the opening night.

02:06:32.800 --> 02:06:34.140
We must be

02:06:34.140 --> 02:06:36.070
a united

02:06:36.070 --> 02:06:40.510
party. It is true of differences.

02:06:40.910 --> 02:06:44.460
She is the grand lady

02:06:44.460 --> 02:06:46.700
of the party. She has a tremendous

02:06:46.700 --> 02:06:48.700
amount of prestige.

02:06:49.140 --> 02:06:50.820
Nobody can be nominated

02:06:50.820 --> 02:06:52.720
without her approval in the Democratic

02:06:52.720 --> 02:06:54.800
Party. And Eleanor Roosevelt

02:06:54.800 --> 02:06:55.900
goes all out.

02:06:56.400 --> 02:06:59.400
I believe that it is

02:06:59.400 --> 02:06:59.440
Thank you.

02:07:01.690 --> 02:07:05.360
that the Democrats come back to power.

02:07:11.580 --> 02:07:12.990
The party is young and vigorous.

02:07:14.320 --> 02:07:19.360
Our party may be the oldest democratic party,

02:07:20.340 --> 02:07:29.700
but our party must live as a young party.

02:07:30.160 --> 02:07:32.580
It must have young leadership.

02:07:32.820 --> 02:07:38.550
This is Franklin Delano Roosevelt.

02:07:38.690 --> 02:07:40.710
Without ever mentioning Adlai Stevenson,

02:07:41.430 --> 02:07:43.190
Eleanor made it clear whom she supported.

02:07:43.330 --> 02:07:46.570
Third appearance before a Democratic National Convention.

02:07:47.280 --> 02:07:50.380
Three days later, he won the nomination for president.

02:07:56.920 --> 02:07:59.380
Eleanor campaigned nonstop for Stevenson.

02:08:00.570 --> 02:08:03.230
But she could not win the election for him.

02:08:05.470 --> 02:08:10.640
They were riding in a limousine to a meeting in Harlem, New York City.

02:08:13.200 --> 02:08:17.500
Cars stopped, and people gathered around the car

02:08:18.230 --> 02:08:21.980
and began to poke their heads right in the window,

02:08:22.000 --> 02:08:25.900
and they recognized Eleanor Roosevelt and perhaps Adlai.

02:08:26.600 --> 02:08:29.250
She said he cringed in the corner.

02:08:30.000 --> 02:08:40.350
said to her, what am I going to say to these people? And she said that I realized then that if

02:08:40.350 --> 02:08:51.030
he didn't know, there was nothing I could tell him. President Eisenhower beat Stevenson by a landslide.

02:08:54.260 --> 02:08:59.840
Eleanor was bitterly disappointed. She hoped, she said, never to be so involved.

02:09:00.030 --> 02:09:01.650
in another political campaign.

02:09:05.780 --> 02:09:09.780
We are now trying to win to the free world

02:09:09.780 --> 02:09:12.260
the uncommitted people of the world

02:09:12.480 --> 02:09:15.710
in Asia, in Africa, and in South America.

02:09:16.820 --> 02:09:20.150
And most of those people are colored people.

02:09:20.760 --> 02:09:24.320
Even in her 70s, Eleanor still sparked controversy.

02:09:25.020 --> 02:09:29.160
And the suggestion that we do not consider...

02:09:33.200 --> 02:09:38.600
makes them feel there is something really radically wrong in everything that we offer them.

02:09:39.180 --> 02:09:41.620
So they'll take a good look at what the communists offer.

02:09:42.150 --> 02:09:43.390
What can we do about this?

02:09:45.620 --> 02:09:47.680
Face it. Face it.

02:09:48.260 --> 02:09:53.050
And realize that we can't afford to have two kinds of sins.

02:09:53.720 --> 02:09:58.840
We must have equal citizenship for anybody in our country.

02:10:00.000 --> 02:10:04.580
conservative newspapers disturbed by her ever more outspoken support of civil rights

02:10:04.580 --> 02:10:11.600
dropped her My Day column. Protesters picketed her appearances. Threats were made on her life.

02:10:14.780 --> 02:10:21.480
In 1958, the Ku Klux Klan learned she was going to speak at a civil rights workshop at the Highlander

02:10:21.480 --> 02:10:27.780
Folk School in Tennessee. The day before she's supposed to go, the FBI contacts her and says,

02:10:27.880 --> 02:10:29.820
Mrs. Roosevelt, we can't guarantee your safety.

02:10:30.730 --> 02:10:33.390
The Klan's put a $25,000 bounty on your head.

02:10:34.180 --> 02:10:36.570
We can't protect you. You can't go.

02:10:38.350 --> 02:10:40.210
Eleanor says, I didn't ask for your protection.

02:10:40.210 --> 02:10:43.410
I appreciate the warning. I have a commitment. I'm going.

02:10:44.150 --> 02:10:45.230
She was relentless.

02:10:45.950 --> 02:10:49.660
She made a statement to the effect that if you don't take a stand,

02:10:50.540 --> 02:10:53.160
you've got to leave the impression that you're cowardly.

02:10:54.850 --> 02:10:56.570
She used the word cowardly.

02:10:57.010 --> 02:10:59.170
So Eleanor flies into the Nashville airport.

02:11:00.000 --> 02:11:06.280
And she's met by this 71-year-old white woman, no Secret Service, no cops, no young muscle

02:11:06.280 --> 02:11:06.960
men around her.

02:11:07.360 --> 02:11:13.140
This elderly white woman picks up a 74-year-old Eleanor Roosevelt, and here they are, they're

02:11:13.140 --> 02:11:14.720
going to stand down the Klan.

02:11:14.940 --> 02:11:25.940
They get in their car, they put a loaded pistol on the front seat between them, and they

02:11:25.940 --> 02:11:28.960
drive up at night through the mountains.

02:11:32.140 --> 02:11:35.540
to this tiny labor school to conduct a workshop

02:11:35.540 --> 02:11:37.220
on how to break the law,

02:11:38.260 --> 02:11:40.760
how to conduct nonviolent civil disobedience.

02:11:43.420 --> 02:11:46.060
And she drove through the Klan to do it.

02:11:46.940 --> 02:11:48.480
She was tough as nails.

02:11:49.400 --> 02:11:50.800
She had made herself tough.

02:11:51.320 --> 02:11:53.040
She was just as tough as FDR,

02:11:53.240 --> 02:11:54.660
just as tough as Theodore Roosevelt.

02:11:55.480 --> 02:11:56.940
She didn't stop,

02:11:57.690 --> 02:11:59.790
because this woman evidently was convinced.

02:12:00.860 --> 02:12:02.720
that she was doing the American thing.

02:12:03.260 --> 02:12:05.040
She was thinking about the future of her country.

02:12:06.520 --> 02:12:09.920
Most Americans never knew of the threats on Eleanor's life.

02:12:11.290 --> 02:12:14.800
And I, Sullivan, for giving us this opportunity.

02:12:15.160 --> 02:12:16.200
I thank you, Mr. Hill.

02:12:16.940 --> 02:12:20.240
By the late 1950s, she seemed to be everywhere.

02:12:21.320 --> 02:12:23.760
Here's to the American Cancer Society.

02:12:24.120 --> 02:12:27.500
A real campaign is being put on by the Soviet Union.

02:12:27.580 --> 02:12:29.970
In politics, in print, on...

02:12:30.000 --> 02:12:31.000
television and radio.

02:12:31.300 --> 02:12:35.900
I wonder if you realize that more than two-thirds of the people in the world are underfed.

02:12:36.230 --> 02:12:39.790
Eleanor used the programs to promote issues she felt were important

02:12:39.880 --> 02:12:42.740
and to make money to help her children.

02:12:42.960 --> 02:12:48.060
Years ago, most people never dreamed of eating margarine, but times have changed.

02:12:48.200 --> 02:12:53.600
Nowadays, you can get a margarine like the new Good Luck, which really tastes delicious.

02:12:54.320 --> 02:12:56.340
That's what I've spread on my toes.

02:12:56.710 --> 02:12:58.880
Good luck. I thoroughly enjoy it.

02:13:09.530 --> 02:13:17.320
I went down for lunch one day, and Maureen Corr, her secretary, said,

02:13:17.380 --> 02:13:19.640
Mrs. Roosevelt, it's a call from California.

02:13:20.700 --> 02:13:21.880
It's Frank Sinatra.

02:13:22.540 --> 02:13:26.360
And Mrs. Roosevelt said, find out who he is, dear, and what he wants.

02:13:27.040 --> 02:13:29.920
It was an invitation to appear at a special.

02:13:30.380 --> 02:13:35.360
There is a Gallup poll taken every year to select the 10 most admired women in the world.

02:13:35.970 --> 02:13:42.760
This year, for the 11th consecutive time, the name at the top of that list is that of a lady whose friendship I treasure very much.

02:13:43.400 --> 02:13:47.640
Ladies and gentlemen, the most admired woman of our time, Mrs. Eleanor Roosevelt.

02:13:50.540 --> 02:13:56.200
If you had one minute to leave one word with, say, 25 million people, what would that word be?

02:13:56.860 --> 02:13:59.160
That one word would be hope.

02:14:00.280 --> 02:14:07.150
Next time you're found with your chin on the ground, there's a lot to be learned.

02:14:07.960 --> 02:14:08.740
So look around.

02:14:09.770 --> 02:14:12.180
Once there was a silly old...

02:14:24.300 --> 02:14:29.980
In the last years of her life, Eleanor enjoyed more than ever the time she could spend at...

02:14:30.000 --> 02:14:37.600
kill. Her house was always filled with people, grandchildren, close friends,

02:14:38.600 --> 02:14:46.800
former New Dealers, visiting dignitaries, and neighbors. She seemed a better mother

02:14:46.800 --> 02:14:54.620
to her grandchildren than she had been to her own. I wasn't beautiful, I wasn't

02:14:54.620 --> 02:14:59.610
social, and my parents really weren't

02:15:00.000 --> 02:15:11.880
sure what to do with me. So my grandmother became my substitute parents and I spent a lot of time

02:15:11.880 --> 02:15:23.480
with her because I felt welcome at her house and she was wonderful to be around.

02:15:30.890 --> 02:15:32.750
She enjoyed life very much.

02:15:32.970 --> 02:15:35.010
She enjoyed parties, giving them.

02:15:35.110 --> 02:15:36.590
She was a wonderful hostess.

02:15:36.730 --> 02:15:38.010
She enjoyed good food.

02:15:38.800 --> 02:15:43.580
In the summer, her cook made the most marvelous frozen daiquiris, which she enjoyed.

02:15:44.710 --> 02:15:46.030
She was great fun.

02:15:46.030 --> 02:15:48.030
She laughed with real gusto.

02:15:49.320 --> 02:15:56.400
We saw a lighter, happier, even though she was older, happier person, a little more relaxed

02:15:56.400 --> 02:15:59.980
and very sure of herself in a gentle way.

02:16:00.000 --> 02:16:12.160
way. More than half a century after her father's death, Eleanor made the journey

02:16:12.160 --> 02:16:18.860
he had promised they would take one day to the Taj Mahal, the monument to eternal love.

02:16:34.440 --> 02:16:37.830
She stayed through the evening, sitting alone in the moonlight.

02:16:40.880 --> 02:16:45.530
As long as I shall live, I shall carry in my mind the beauty of the Taj.

02:16:47.270 --> 02:16:53.330
At last I know why my father always said it was the one thing he wanted us to see together.

02:17:00.000 --> 02:17:03.790
By 1962, Eleanor's age was catching up with her.

02:17:04.990 --> 02:17:08.220
For the first time in her life, she admitted she was tired.

02:17:09.190 --> 02:17:12.870
That summer, she didn't seem her old self.

02:17:13.990 --> 02:17:17.670
There were some engagements she actually didn't want to go to.

02:17:18.560 --> 02:17:21.390
At night, she would have sweats.

02:17:22.760 --> 02:17:29.040
Then she started to bleed, and she was forced, really, to go to New York City to specialists.

02:17:30.510 --> 02:17:33.950
Doctors found she was suffering from bone marrow tuberculosis.

02:17:36.960 --> 02:17:40.040
She did persuade them to let her go back to her apartment.

02:17:43.380 --> 02:17:44.860
She was just very tired.

02:17:45.890 --> 02:17:47.410
She didn't want to fight anymore.

02:17:51.880 --> 02:17:55.220
Eleanor Roosevelt died on November 7, 1962.

02:17:55.460 --> 02:17:59.230
She was 78 years old.

02:18:02.240 --> 02:18:11.340
All government offices and all overseas installations were ordered by the president to fly the American flag at half mass.

02:18:13.490 --> 02:18:26.030
It was acknowledgment of what we already knew from the polls and from stories that would come from little villages and hamlets all over the world, that she was the most admired woman in the world.

02:18:26.940 --> 02:18:29.880
The world has suffered irreparable loss.

02:18:37.160 --> 02:18:41.000
Eleanor Roosevelt had a sense of discovery.

02:18:43.990 --> 02:18:45.410
She kept discovering herself.

02:18:47.610 --> 02:18:48.390
She kept growing.

02:18:48.860 --> 02:18:53.160
And I don't think she knew that she would become the Eleanor Roosevelt as she did.

02:18:58.500 --> 02:18:59.760
Transcended politics.

02:19:00.000 --> 02:19:07.560
and all religions, she was recognized as the best that America could be.

02:19:13.200 --> 02:19:17.760
This popularity of Eleanor Roosevelt was quite extraordinary.

02:19:19.670 --> 02:19:26.860
You look at her life, there's no way that you can record legislation that she was responsible for.

02:19:28.910 --> 02:19:29.970
It isn't any...

02:19:30.000 --> 02:19:35.280
Many of the ways in which we normally peg a person's recognition,

02:19:35.280 --> 02:19:42.160
it is because of who she was.

02:19:44.680 --> 02:19:48.380
And who she was, the vibrations of it continue to echo.

